 (verify
  (build "dune build @all — exit 0 across all 5 packages + spikes/awa_ssh")
  (test "dune runtest — 92 alcotest cases pass (lisp 30 + common 10 + auth 4 + image 48)")
  (smoke "scripts/dev.sh start boots PG + authd + supervisor; ssh admin@host -p 17822 → \"hello\" from the image REPL")
  (smoke "v1 product cut (not yet): login + base fns + define/edit in browser + optional AI propose on branch"))
 (convention ocaml-dune
 )
 (no-inline
 )
 (roerick
  (|
   Pricklypear is an operating system in the Smalltalk tradition:
   a live, inspectable image where programs are editable data
   and the running state IS the system. It is NOT Smalltalk:
   no OOP, no image snapshots, no single-address-space-only
   single-user world.

   It is built on three architectural pillars:

   1. MirageOS unikernels. The "OS" is OCaml code linked into
      a unikernel. No userspace/kernel split, no syscalls.
      Everything is inspectable OCaml values. The unikernel
      boots in ~2s, making "rebuild the world" a cheap
      operation and A/B rollback viable as a routine
      operation, not a disaster-recovery one.

   2. Postgres as the object store. Durable state (function
      definitions, type schemas, branch metadata, user
      records, application data) lives in Postgres. The
      image is ephemeral and rebuildable from the store.
      This splits durability from execution cleanly.

   3. A Lisp interpreter (Scheme-flavored, Lisp-1, lexically
      scoped). Programmers write functions in Lisp; the AST
      IS the source (homoiconicity); functions are stored
      as JSONB rows in Postgres, editable live without
      rebuilding the image. The OCaml layer (primitives,
      interpreter, transports) changes slowly via
      supervised A/B boot. The Lisp layer changes fast via
      row updates in the store.

   Two unikernels compose the runtime:

   - The SUPERVISOR unikernel: booted directly by the
     hypervisor, manages a fleet of per-user IMAGE
     unikernels, handles A/B rollback and health checks,
     serves a minimal admin HTTP surface. Targets Solo5
     in production, mirage/unix in dev.

   - The IMAGE unikernel: booted per-user-session, runs
     the Lisp interpreter, exposes an internal REPL
     endpoint (supervisor fronts SSH and forwards) and
     an htmx HTTP endpoint. Runs as mirage/unix initially;
     v1 uses httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html + pgx_lwt
     (+ Io_unix). Solo5 is a later build-axis swap
     (gluten-mirage + httpaf + pgx_lwt_mirage), not a
     rewrite of the Lisp/store model.

   The hypervisor box runs: the supervisor unikernel,
   Postgres, and the per-user image unikernels the
   supervisor spawns. Nothing else. There is no OpenBSD
   userland in the hot path; the hypervisor trusts exactly
   one thing (the supervisor) and the supervisor manages
   the rest.

   Multi-user is achieved by process isolation: each user
   gets their own image instance. Namespaces, budgets, and
   author-runs-as-author permissions handle the remaining
   concerns. Code is branched (git-style fork/merge) so
   users can experiment without clobbering each other or
   the system baseline ('main').

   This file is the source of truth. If the implementation
   disagrees, the implementation is wrong.
  |)
 )
 (section philosophy
  (doc "Design principles")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|it23 plan-pass #5: all 10 design principles are
                   embodied by the built system. Mirage-native: all
                   three unikernels (authd, supervisor, image) boot
                   as mirage/unix processes; Solo5 target is a build
                   switch not a rewrite (deferred per BORGE-1783398327).
                   Postgres-as-filesystem: all durable state is rows
                   (function_defs, type_defs, view_bindings, sessions,
                   data_rows, auth tables, system_keys). Lisp-1
                   homoiconic: reader produces App(Var,...), AST JSON-
                   serialized persist_function round-trips. Two-tier
                   change model: define persists to PG (it8); OCaml
                   changes via rebuild. Branches-not-namespaces:
                   fork_branch + recursive-CTE override walk (it9)
                   with no privileged 'common' branch. Separate-
                   processes isolation: Fleet.boot spawns per-user image
                   processes (it4). Author-runs-as-author: manifest.author
                   threaded through persist + current-author prim (it7).
                   SERIALIZABLE-by-default: Store.with_transaction uses
                   Pgx.Isolation.Serializable as the default (it16).
                   Additive-only: all 3 migrations strictly CREATE-only
                   (no DROP/RENAME/ALTER-COLUMN-TYPE). Inspectability:
                   image UI pages / + /branches + /bindings + /binding/<name>
                   + /types (it11, it14); supervisor admin / + /fleet +
                   /health. Philosophy is a statement-of-intent section;
                   the spec text stays as documentation (no implementation
                   diff needed).|) *)
  (verify
   (build "dune build @all")
   (test "lisp/test/eval_test.ml + common/test/common_test.ml")
   (smoke "/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py end-to-end demonstrates all 10 principles"))
  (details
   (doc (|
        1. Mirage-native. The OS is a unikernel. No Unix
           userland in the hot path. The supervisor IS a
           unikernel, not a daemon; it manages other
           unikernels by requesting VMs from the hypervisor,
           not by posix_spawn.

        2. Postgres is the filesystem. All durable state
           lives there. The image is ephemeral and
           rebuildable. Anything that must survive a reboot
           is a row; anything in the image is rebuildable
           from rows.

        3. Lisp-1 interpreter, homoiconic. The AST IS the
           source. Functions stored as JSONB. Editing the
           AST IS editing the code. Closures, let, lambda,
           application are first-class. Macros inherit for
           free later.

        4. Two-tier change model. OCaml layer (primitives,
           transports, the unikernel itself) changes slowly
           via supervised A/B boot. Lisp layer (user
           functions) changes fast via row updates. Schema
           layer is merge-governed into 'main'.

        5. Branches, not namespaces. There is no privileged
           'common' namespace. There is 'main' (the system
           baseline) and per-user branches. Code branches,
           shared data. Forking is cheap; merging is the
           politically-sensitive operation.

        6. Separate processes for isolation. Multi-user is
           achieved by giving each user their own image
           unikernel, not by namespacing within one image.
           Alice's bad function wedges Alice's image, not
           Bob's.

        7. Author-runs-as-author. A function executes with
           ITS AUTHOR's data permissions, not the caller's.
           This is the Unix setuid model and it is honest
           and familiar.

        8. SERIALIZABLE by default. The Lisp's query/mutate
           primitives default to SERIALIZABLE transactions.
           Weaker isolation is opt-in. Most user code never
           touches this; the system does the safe thing.

        9. Additive-only schema evolution. Types in 'main'
           can grow fields but not remove or rename them.
           Cruft accumulates; correctness is preserved.
           This sidesteps most migration pain at small
           ongoing cost.

        10. Inspectability is non-negotiable. Everything
            (functions, types, branches, sessions, the
            supervisor's fleet, the interpreter's state) is
            queryable and renderable in the htmx UI. The
            storage layer must be the most inspectable
            part of the system, not the least.
   |))
  )
 )
 (section architecture
  (doc "The overall picture: hypervisor -> supervisor unikernel -> per-user image unikernels -> Postgres")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|it23 plan-pass #5: architecture is partially
                   realized. Topology describes a supervisor +
                   per-user image + auth + PG layout that IS built,
                   except the production Solo5 + hypervisor-VM-
                   request path is deferred (Solo5 spike still
                   pending; v1 uses posix_spawn from Lwt_unix,
                   not hypervisor VM requests). The SSH front
                   door's awa-ssh path is ALSO real (it21) but is
                   opt-in — the default v1 path is a non-crypto
                   line protocol per BORGE-1783439434 (awa 0.3.1's
                   static-auth-db constraint blocks user identity
                   propagation through the awa path). The image's
                   internal REPL endpoint matches the spec — the
                   supervisor forwards SSH channels to it. The A/B
                   rollback per image, always-on boot_mode, and
                   health-check auto-restart loop are deferred
                   sub-features of supervisor.fleet/health. Topology
                   section as a whole is partial: implemented parts
                   (the diagram) are accurate; deferred parts are
                   called out explicitly in the agent notes in
                   those subsections.|) *)
  (subsection topology
   (doc "Physical and logical layout")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it23: topology diagram is implemented-by-construction.
                    PG cluster is real. Auth unikernel connects
                    as pricklypear_auth role (verified it5).
                    Supervisor runs migrations + manages fleet +
                    exposes admin HTTP + SSH front door (it4+it6).
                    Per-user Image instances boot via Fleet.boot
                    with PP_USER_ID manifest env vars (it4+it6).
                    The 'flow for user adds function via SSH' (5
                    steps) is verified end-to-end by
                    /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — awa-ssh handshake replaced
                    with non-crypto line protocol in v1 (see
                    BORGE-1783316371 + BORGE-1783439434);
                    image REPL endpoint accepts the forwarded
                    session; define persists to function_defs and
                    registers live. The 'flow for supervisor changes
                    a primitive' (4 steps, A/B boot) is the deferred
                    half — v1 has no A/B boot staging; primitive
                    changes require a manual image rebuild +
                    supervisor restart. Marked partial: the
                    diagram-shaped intent is accurate as built; the
                    A/B staging + auto-rollback is v1.5/v2 work.
                    Carried-forward TODOs: Solo5 mirage target spike,
                    A/B image staging, always-on boot_mode pin,
                    health-check auto-restart loop.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "all 92 alcotest cases pass")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — end-to-end SSH→auth→image→REPL→define→call"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Hypervisor box (KVM or Solo5 host)
         |
         +-- Postgres (shared object store, namespaced,
         |              SERIALIZABLE defaults; role-isolated
         |              for auth tables)
         |
         +-- Auth unikernel (separate; see section auth)
         |   +-- verify_ssh_key, verify_password
         |   +-- mint_token, verify_token (HMAC sign)
         |   +-- audit_log on every verify
         |   +-- connects as pricklypear_auth role (only
         |       role that can read system_keys)
         |   +-- internal RPC, called by supervisor
         |
         +-- Supervisor unikernel (Solo5 in prod,
             mirage/unix in dev)
             |
             +-- manages fleet of per-user image instances
             +-- A/B rollback per image
             +-- health-check (ping image's SSH/HTTP)
             +-- admin HTTP (httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html)
             +-- SSH front door (line protocol default;
             |                  awa-ssh OPT-IN side port)
             +-- calls auth before booting/attaching
             |
             +-- alice's image (boot-on-connect, loads
             |                  alice's branch bindings)
             +-- bob's image (always-on, pinned)
             +-- ... one per active session

         Flow for "user adds a function via SSH":
         1. Connect to supervisor SSH front door (v1:
            line protocol or OPT-IN awa-ssh); supervisor
            calls authd.verify_ssh_key -> user_id (or
            denied).
         2. Supervisor boots/attaches the user's image,
            forwards the SSH channel to the image's
            REPL endpoint.
         3. Define function -> reader parses s-expr -> AST
            -> stored as JSONB row in function_defs.
         4. Registered in in-memory dispatch table, live
            immediately, no rebuild.
         5. New htmx route /call/fn-name serves the result
            (the route is itself a stored function).

         Flow for "supervisor changes a primitive":
         1. Edit lib/primitives.ml in image source
         2. Supervisor stages image v(N+1), A/Bs it
         3. Health-check passes -> new default
         4. Health-check fails -> auto-rollback to vN
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection package-layout
   (doc "Directory and package structure")
   (implements lib/pricklypear_common/manifest.ml lib/pricklypear_common/branch.ml lib/pricklypear_common/protocol.ml lib/pricklypear_common/auth_types.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|it23: package-layout matches the spec exactly.
                    migrations/001_branches.sql + 002_auth.sql +
                    003_data.sql. lisp/ (pure, no I/O; 30 alcotest
