                    lib/{fleet,health,process,store,ssh,ssh_awa,
                    ssh_awa_shims,ssh_keys,auth_client,admin,boot}.ml
                    + bin/supervisor.ml. auth/ at lib/{verify,tokens,
                    admin_api,store,http,boot}.ml + bin/authd.ml +
                    test/auth_test.ml (4 alcotest cases). proto/ in spec
                    is common/ in the actual code (the boot_manifest,
                    health protocol, auth_types modules live there).
                    scripts/dev.sh orchestrates PG + authd + supervisor.
                    Spec text is accurate as the actual code structure,
                    modulo proto/ -> common/ rename.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "scripts/dev.sh start - boots PG + authd + supervisor on the documented directory layout"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         pricklypear/                    the whole system
         |
         +-- migrations/                 SQL, shared data
         |   +-- 001_branches.sql        code store: branches,
         |                               function_defs, type_defs,
         |                               view_bindings, sessions
         |   +-- 002_auth.sql            users, credentials, sessions,
         |                               auth_log, system_keys
         |                               (NOT data tables — those come
         |                               from type_defs accepted to main)
         |
         +-- proto/                      shared format defs
         |   +-- boot_manifest.json      per-user boot config
         |   +-- health.proto            health-check format
         |
         +-- lisp/                       the interpreter
         |                               PURE, no I/O dep
         |   +-- lib/ast.ml              expr + value types
         |   +-- lib/reader.ml           s-expr -> ast
         |   +-- lib/env.ml              lexical env
         |   +-- lib/eval.ml             the evaluator
         |   +-- bin/repl.ml             dev REPL, no image
         |   +-- test/eval_test.ml       alcotest, no svc
         |
         +-- image/                      Mirage unikernel
         |                               per-user instance
         |   +-- lib/store.ml            PG client, branch
         |   +-- lib/primitives.ml       ~20 impure fns
         |   +-- lib/session.ml         per-conn REPL env
         |   +-- lib/repl_endpoint.ml    internal REPL protocol
         |                               (supervisor forwards SSH
         |                               channel here; not full SSH)
         |   +-- lib/http.ml             httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html
         |   +-- lib/crud.ml             data_rows CRUD prims
         |   +-- lib/lineedit.ml         readline stub (linenoise later)
         |   +-- lib/boot.ml             reads manifest
         |   +-- bin/unikernel.ml        mirage/unix entry
         |
         +-- supervisor/                 unikernel, root
         |                               manages fleet
         |   +-- lib/fleet.ml            per-user instances
         |   +-- lib/health.ml           ping images
         |   +-- lib/process.ml         boot/kill (Solo5
         |                               VM-request model)
         |   +-- lib/store.ml           PG (manifests, sessions)
         |                               — Lwt via pgx_lwt + Io_unix
         |   +-- lib/ssh.ml             line-protocol front door
         |   +-- lib/ssh_awa.ml         OPT-IN awa-ssh side port
         |   +-- lib/auth_client.ml      RPC client to authd
         |                               (verify_ssh_key, tokens)
         |   +-- lib/admin.ml           httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html,
         |                               fleet dashboard routes
         |   +-- bin/supervisor.ml       entry point
         |
         +-- auth/                       the auth unikernel
         |                               (separate; see section auth)
         |   +-- lib/verify.ml           verify_ssh_key, verify_password
         |   +-- lib/tokens.ml           mint, verify_token, HMAC sign
         |   +-- lib/admin_api.ml        create_user, add_credential (admin)
         |   +-- lib/store.ml           PG (users, credentials, sessions,
         |                               auth_log, system_keys) — own PG role
         |   +-- lib/http.ml             httpaf-lwt-unix, internal RPC
         |   +-- bin/authd.ml            entry point
         |   +-- test/verify_test.ml    crypto tests, no services
         |
         +-- common/                     shared OCaml lib
         |   +-- lib/manifest.ml         boot manifest type
         |   +-- lib/branch.ml           branch ref type
         |   +-- lib/protocol.ml         health msg types
         |   +-- lib/auth_types.ml      user, credential, token types
         |                               (shared supervisor <-> auth)
         |
         +-- config/                     infra config
         +-- scripts/                    dev helpers
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection transport-choices
   (doc "Why each transport + framework choice")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it23: transport choices are realized as documented,
                    with the same SSH caveat: the spec text says
                    'awa-ssh listens on :22' which IS the design; v1
                    uses a non-crypto line protocol on :PP_SSH_PORT as
                    the default (deferred from it6) and the real awa-ssh
                    path is opt-in on PP_AWA_SSH_PORT (it21) — see
                    BORGE-1783439434 for the awa 0.3.1 constraint.
                    htmx via httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html IS realized on
                    both supervisor (admin.ml) and image (http.ml) —
                    pages use HTML.Hx.* htmx attributes (it11 UI
                    pages, it14 render-html prim). Admin HTTP routes
                    are ~3 currently (GET /, /fleet, /health). Postgres
                    via pgx_lwt with custom Io_unix shim
                    (BORGE-1783389549). SERIALIZABLE-by-default via
                    Store.with_transaction (it16). ONE v1 stack on both
                    unikernels (httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html + pgx_lwt)
                    — confirmed; doctrinal details rewritten it31 to
                    match. No Dream. Marked partial: default SSH is
                    still line-protocol; real awa default depends on
                    awa 0.5.x (BORGE-1783439434); Solo5 transport swap
                    deferred.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "scripts/dev.sh start boots the documented stack"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SSH (supervisor front door — see image.ssh-interface):
           - v1 default: non-crypto line protocol on
             PP_SSH_PORT; OPT-IN real awa-ssh on
             PP_AWA_SSH_PORT (see BORGE-1783439434)
           - Supervisor authenticates via authd
             verify_ssh_key (NOT wyo.auth), forwards to
             image's internal REPL endpoint
           - Image does NOT run full SSH, just a line
             protocol on an internal port

         htmx (image interface):
           - v1: httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html (no Dream).
             Future Solo5 path: gluten-mirage + httpaf
             (there is no httpaf-mirage package on opam).
           - Routes ARE functions in the store
           - pure-html provides HTML.Hx.* htmx attribute
             support (the type-safe htmx attrs we want)

         Admin (supervisor):
           - v1: httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html (same stack
             as image)
           - ~3 routes live today (/, /fleet, /health);
             kill/rollback routes deferred
           - Hand-written small router

         Postgres:
           - v1: pgx_lwt + custom Io_unix shim (POSIX).
             pgx_lwt_mirage is installed but needs a real
             Tcpip.Stack.V4V6 — Solo5/v1.5 axis only.
           - SERIALIZABLE by default for mutations
           - LISTEN/NOTIFY available for cache
             invalidation later
           - Lwt throughout unikernels (no Eio in the
             hot path; Eio only for pure-unix dev tools)

         ONE stack on both unikernels in v1:
         httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html + pgx_lwt(+Io_unix).
         No Dream. Future Solo5 stack swap stays behind
         the same Store/Http interfaces:
         gluten-mirage + httpaf + pgx_lwt_mirage.
         Reasoning:
         - Keep one mental model across supervisor/image
         - htmx is rendering chrome; shell/REPL is primary
         - pure-html gives HTML.Hx.* without Dream
    |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section object-model
  (doc "Typed records in Postgres, schema declared in Lisp, SQL-via-primitives, no ORM")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|it23 plan-pass #5: object-model v1 foundation is
                   built, the typed-field half is deferred. WHAT'S
                   REAL: '(deftype 'name 'field1 'field2 ...)' prim
                   (it14) inserts into type_defs (fields stored as
                   JSON array of field-name strings — not typed).
                   Data lives in the shared 'data_rows' table (one
                   row per instance, no per-type PG table is emitted
                   — see migration 003 + BORGE-1783433175).
                   CRUD prims are implemented: (create 'type
                   "{fields-json}") (it13), (get 'type id) (it13),
                   (update 'type id "{fields-json}") (it13),
                   (delete 'type id) (it13), (rows 'type [limit])
                   (it13), (select-for-update 'type id) (it17),
                   (update-field 'type id "field" "value") (it18).
                   The /types htmx page lists type_defs (it14).
                   NO ORM anywhere — all SQL is hand-written in
                   image/lib/store.ml + image/lib/crud.ml.
                   WHAT'S DEFERRED: typed field declarations
                   (:string/:int/:bool/:ref user/:array (ref user)/
                   :timestamptz/:json) — type_defs stores field NAME
                   lists only, no field TYPE. Runtime field-level
                   validation against main's schema is unbuilt
                   (v1 data_rows accepts any jsonb fields bag for
                   any type_name). The 'system creates or ALTERs
                   underlying Postgres table on declaration/merge'
                   machinery (the type-merge-governance flow) is
                   NOT built (v1 uses the single shared data_rows
                   table to sidestep the emitted-DDL complexity).
                   '(query 'event where: ...)' parameterized SQL
                   suger is not built (v1 has (rows 'type [limit])
                   instead — returns all rows of a type, no WHERE).
                   ':ref user' first-class values with type tags
                   (lazy or eager deref) are not built — v1 refs
                   are just plain strings/UUIDs. Marked partial:
                   the 'objects are typed records + no methods +
                   CRUD prims + no ORM' shape is implemented; the
                   typed-field + query-sugar + schema-validation
                   are v1.5/v2 work.|) *)
  (verify
   (build "dune build @all")
   (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml 48 alcotest cases incl. CRUD + deftype + dict + render-html")
   (smoke "/tmp/pp_crud_it13.py + /tmp/pp_types_it14.py + /tmp/pp_sfu_it17.py + /tmp/pp_uf_it18.py"))
  (details
   (doc (|
        An "object" is a typed record with an id, a set of
        named typed fields, stored in Postgres. There are
        NO methods on objects. Methods are functions in the
        store that take an object id as argument. This is
        functions + data, not OOP, matching the poohstack
        shape.

        Types are DECLARED IN LISP, not in SQL migrations:

        (deftype event
          (fields
            (title :string)
            (start :timestamptz)
            (end :timestamptz)
            (owner :ref user)
            (attendees :array (ref user))))

        The declaration lives in function_defs-equivalent
        table (type_defs, branched like code). On
        declaration/merge to main, the system creates or
        ALTERs the underlying Postgres table.

        The type is itself inspectable data: the htmx UI
        can render "list all types, show fields, browse
        instances" nearly free. This is the Smalltalk-image
        browser feeling, achieved via a metamodel rather
        than via live mutable objects.

        CRUD primitives (real OCaml, typed at boundary):
          (create 'event {...})      -> row insert
          (get 'event id)            -> row fetch
          (update 'event id {...})   -> row update
          (delete 'event id)         -> row delete
          (query 'event where: ...)  -> parameterized SQL
          (select-for-update ...)    -> explicit row lock

        References are first-class values: a :ref user
        field holds an id + type tag. Dereferencing is
        explicit: (get 'user (event-owner ev)).

        The Lisp still has plain values (int, string, bool,
        lambda, list, ref) for logic that doesn't touch the
        DB. Pure functions for "does this event conflict?"
        take event values, return bool, no DB. The DB and
        the Lisp have a clean seam.

        NO ORM. SQL is the query language. (query ...)
        desugars to parameterized SQL; the primitive layer
        parses results into typed Lisp values.
   |))
  )
  (subsection type-system
   (doc "Field types and reference semantics")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (|it23 plan-pass #5: type-system is planned (v1 has
                    NO typed-field implementation). type_defs stores
                    field NAME lists only (e.g. 'name email age' as
                    a JSON array of strings) — no field TYPE
                    declarations. The 7 field types in the spec
                    text (:string, :int, :bool, :timestamptz,
                    :ref <type>, :array <type>, :json) are
                    described-as-design but have no runtime
                    enforcement: any jsonb fields bag is accepted
                    for any type_name by v1 data_rows. v1.5 plan:
                    extend (deftype ...) syntax to take field-type
                    pairs, persist field types in type_defs.fields,
                    and run runtime field-level validation on
                    create/update. v2 plan: ':ref user' as a
                    first-class tagged value (eager deref first;
                    lazy stub-ref later if perf requires).
                    No inheritance, no subtyping is preserved as a
                    principle via storage.no-inheritance (which
                    IS implemented — recursive-CTE override walk).
                    Marked planned because no typed-field code
                    exists as of it23|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Field types (initial cut, grow as needed):
           :string         text
           :int            int8
           :bool           bool
           :timestamptz    timestamptz
           :ref <type>     uuid FK to <type>_table.id
           :array <type>   <type>[] (homogeneous)
           :json           jsonb (schemaless escape hatch)

         A (ref user) value is a tagged id: the value
         carries its target type so the interpreter can
         verify derefs at call time. Dereferencing returns
         the full record or a stub-ref that lazy-loads on
         field access (decision deferred; start eager).

         Cycles in the type graph are allowed (user has
         :ref calendar; calendar has :ref user) because
         refs hold ids, not nested values.

         No inheritance, no subtyping. Composition via
         references, not via type hierarchies. This matches
         the poohstack (relational, no ORM, no inheritance)
         and avoids the Postgres INHERITS trap (see
         storage.no-inheritance).
    |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section branching
  (doc "Branches as the fundamental organizational primitive: code branches, shared data, fork/merge")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|Section overall `partial` as of it17/it18 plan-pass:
    fork = implemented, merge = partial, schema-authority = partial.
    Per-subsection status below carries the per-feature detail.|) *)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Branches are to Pricklypear what files are to
        Unix: the fundamental organizational primitive.
        Everything is a branch; everything else is built
        on top of that.

        KEY DISTINCTION: branches are for CODE, not data.
        - BRANCHED (lives in branches): function
          definitions, type schemas, view/route bindings.
          Each row keyed by (branch_id, kind, name).
        - SHARED (lives in main data tables): the actual
          records those functions operate on. Event rows
          are NOT branched. This is git's model applied
          religiously.

        This split is critical. It means:
        - Alice's experimental calendar branch can render
          against her REAL events. She forks the code,
          not the data.
        - Forking is cheap: copy code-rows (a few hundred
          inserts). Negligible cost.
        - Multi-user data races on code are gone: each
          branch is owned by one editor.
        - Cache coherence is trivial: invalidate only on
          merge, which is rare and explicit.
        - The 'common namespace' political problem is just
          git's merge-upstream politics.

        A branch is identified by (owner, name):
          main            the system baseline, root
          alice/main      alice's fork of main
          alice/experiment alice's fork of alice/main
        Each branch has a parent. Forking = create new
        branch row + copy code-rows from parent.

        Effective definition lookup (CLOS override, not
        Postgres union): walk up the parent chain, return
        first (closest) definition found. Plain SELECT
        against function_defs WHERE branch_id IN (chain).
        No Postgres INHERITS (see storage.no-inheritance).
   |))
  )
  (subsection ast-nodes
   (doc "Design: addressable AST nodes as substrate for surgical edits and AI patches")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7: NEW planned design subsection,
     elevating BORGE-1783398327 out of bugs-only limbo. Not required
     for v1 product cut (whole-function define/edit/AI propose is
     enough). Captures the 'nodes as substrate, named branchable
     subtrees as versioning unit' proposal so future plan/make work
     has a home. No schema migration until explicitly planned.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TODAY: two trees, two models
         - Branch tree: rows in branches, fork/merge,
           recursive CTE lookup, unit = whole function
         - AST tree: JSONB blob in function_defs.body,
           in-memory eval walk, no node ids, edit =
           replace whole body

         PROPOSAL (design only):
         Give AST nodes identity so AI/human tools can
         patch subexpressions without rewriting whole
         functions, while keeping branches as the
         versioning model over named subtrees.

         Sketch (non-normative until a later plan pass):
           nodes(id, branch_id, kind, payload, parent_id,
                 position, ...)
         function_defs may become a view/root pointer
         over a node tree, or remain a cache of the
         root expression.

         Non-goals for first product cut:
         - Do not block function-editor or ai-authoring
           tools on this redesign
         - Whole-function propose/edit ships first
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection fork
   (doc "Forking a branch")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it9 (2026-07-06); hardened with a
     SERIALIZABLE transaction in it16. The 3-step implementation
     matches the spec text below exactly: INSERT branches row,
     INSERT function_defs copy, INSERT type_defs copy, INSERT
     view_bindings copy. All 4 statements run in a single
     `Store.with_transaction` (it16) — atomic, pgx-rolled-back on
     exception. The primitive is a normal Lwt-typed prim registered
     via `register_session_control_prims` (it12); its return value
     is the new branch UUID. Smoke test `/tmp/pp_fork_it9.py` exercises
     the full lifecycle; `/tmp/pp_override_it9.py` exercises the
     branch isolation half of fork semantics.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         (fork 'main 'alice/experiment)

         Implementation:
         1. INSERT INTO branches (id, parent_id, owner,
            name, ...) VALUES (new_uuid, main_id, 'alice',
            'experiment', ...)
         2. INSERT INTO function_defs (branch_id, name,
            body, author, updated_at)
            SELECT new_uuid, name, body, author,
            updated_at FROM function_defs
            WHERE branch_id = parent_id
         3. Similarly for type_defs, view_bindings

         Cost: O(num code rows in parent). For ~100 fns
         and ~20 types, a few hundred row inserts. Lands
         in well under a second.

         Forking does NOT copy data table rows. The
         forked branch's functions operate on the same
         shared event rows as the parent.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — 48 alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_fork_it9.py — fork creates + copies function_defs")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_override_it9.py — override-walk + branch isolation"))
  )
  (subsection merge
   (doc "Merging a branch into its parent")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it10 (2026-07-06); hardened with a
     SERIALIZABLE transaction in it16. The current implementation
     does the basic case: INSERT INTO function_defs SELECT ... FROM
     function_defs WHERE branch_id=source ON CONFLICT (branch_id,
     name) DO UPDATE — full-source-to-target upsert of function_defs.
     Returns `merged N rows` count via INSERT RETURNING.
     Policy enforced at the Lisp prim layer (session.ml):
     admin-only merges TO system branches (owner="system"),
     per-user can merge into their own branches freely. Verified
     end-to-end by /tmp/pp_merge_it10.py (both happy-path and
     policy-rejection).

     NOT YET IMPLEMENTED (deferred — these are nicer-to-haves over
     the basic merge):
     (1) diff-based merge (only fns whose updated_at > parent's
         last_synced_with_child, OR whose name was added in child).
     (2) per-function conflict detection (parent ALSO modified same
         name since fork → conflict surfacing).
     (3) type_defs schema-merge governance (only main accepts
         schema changes via migration-governed merge).
     (4) the real htmx inspector UI for per-function conflict
         resolution.

     Section marked `partial` until at least (1) and (2) land; (3)
     and (4) are explicitly v2+ features.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         (merge 'alice/experiment 'alice/main)

         Policy:
         - Default: admin-only merges to main,
           per-user can merge into their own branches
           freely.
         - Expandable later (PR/review flow).

         Implementation:
         1. Compute diff: code rows in alice/experiment
            whose updated_at > parent's last_sync_with_child,
            OR whose name was added in child.
         2. For each changed function, apply to parent.
            Conflict if parent ALSO modified same name
            since fork. Per-function conflict (coarse, not
            line-level): pick a version, or write a new one.
         3. Schema changes (type_defs) are merge-governed
            separately: only main accepts schema changes.
            A branch can propose; main accepts via a
            special merge that runs the migration.

         The merge needs a real htmx UI (in the inspector):
         list changed functions, show diffs (whole function
         bodies since functions are atomic units), let user
         resolve per-function.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — 48 alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_merge_it10.py — merge basic upsert + admin-only policy"))
  )
  (subsection schema-authority
   (doc "Types in main, branches read against main's schema")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|Partially implemented as of it14 (2026-07-06): the
     `(deftype 'name 'field1 ...)` prim lands type declarations into
     the `type_defs` table on the current branch; `Store.list_types`
     walks the recursive-CTE override chain so ancestor type
     declarations are visible to descendant branches (the read side
     of "branches read against main's schema"). The /types system page
     surfaces them in the UI.

     NOT YET IMPLEMENTED (deferred — explicitly v2+):
     (1) the migration-governed schema-change accept flow ("only
         main accepts schema changes via a special merge that runs
         the migration"). Currently any admin-author on the system
         branch can run (deftype ...) directly.
     (2) schema_version bump + cache-invalidation broadcast on
         schema change (LISTEN/NOTIFY or schema_version check).
     (3) governance policy for proposed-vs-accepted schema changes
         across non-system branches.

     The read side (recursive-CTE type lookup) IS implemented and
     spec-text-aligned. Marked `partial` until at least the
     migration-governed merge-to-main accept flow and the
     schema_version invalidation hook land.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Schema authority is 'main'. Branches CANNOT change
         the underlying table shape directly; they propose
         schema changes which only take effect when merged
         to main.

         When main accepts a schema change:
         1. Run migration on shared data table
            (additive-only: see migrations.additive-only)
         2. Bump schema_version
         3. All branches see new shape on next function
            lookup (cache invalidation: schema_version
            check, or LISTEN/NOTIFY)

         Branch code that ignores new fields: fine, reads
         subset. Branch code that expects old fields:
         errors at runtime (additive-only means removed
         fields don't happen, but renamed/reused-semantic
         fields could; document expectations).

         This makes type_defs the slow-changing,
         merge-governed layer; function_defs the
         fast-changing, per-branch layer. Two tiers of
         change, mirroring the OCaml-slow/Lisp-fast split.
   |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section lisp-interpreter
  (doc "Pure Scheme-flavored Lisp-1 interpreter, AST-as-source, primitives layer")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|Section overall `partial` as of it18 plan-pass:
    ast = implemented, reader = implemented, eval = partial
    (Lwt-typed eval shipped it12; the per-call step-budget / cooperative
    stepping described in eval subsection text is deferred),
    primitives = implemented (the ~21 prim set + dict prims + render-html
    + CRUD + select-for-update + deftype + branching control prims).|) *)
  (subsection ast
   (doc "The expr and value types")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|lisp/lib/ast.ml ships exactly the type schema documented
     below (Var/Lit/App/Let/Letrec/If/Lambda/Prim/Seq/Quote on the expr
     side; VInt/VStr/VBool/VNil/VPair/VLambda/VPrim/VRef/VList on the
     value side). AST serializes to/from JSON via Ast.expr_of_json /
     expr_to_json (Yojson) for storage in function_defs.body::jsonb.
     Homoiconicity preserved (the reader produces the same shape eval
     consumes; printer round-trips). Closed value type universe — no VDict
     was ever introduced (v1 used VStr-JSON + dict prims instead; see
     BORGE-1783432745).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         type expr =
           | Var of string
           | Lit of value
           | App of expr * expr list
           | Let of string * expr * expr
           | Letrec of (string * expr) list * expr
           | If of expr * expr * expr
           | Lambda of string list * expr
           | Prim of string * expr list
           | Seq of expr * expr
           | Quote of expr                 (* for macros later *)

         type value =
           | VInt of int64
           | VStr of string
           | VBool of bool
           | VNil
           | VPair of value * value           (* cons cells *)
           | VLambda of string list * expr * env
           | VPrim of string                  (* primitive name *)
           | VRef of type_name * id           (* tagged ref into store *)
           | VList of value list              (* sugar for nested pairs *)

         The AST IS the source (homoiconicity). Stored as
         JSONB in function_defs.body. The reader (s-expr
         parser) produces expr values; the printer
         round-trips them. No parser/printer asymmetry.

         Closures capture their defining env (lexical
         scope). VLambda carries env. No dynamic scope.

         VRef carries its target type so the interpreter
         can verify derefs at call time before querying
         the store.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection reader
   (doc "S-expression reader: source text -> AST")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|lisp/lib/reader.ml. Recognizes standard s-expr
     surface syntax: (foo bar baz), 'symbol, "string", 123, #t, #f,
     (). Comments `;` to end-of-line. Special forms (lambda/let/letrec/
     if/define/quote/fork/merge/route/checkout/deftype) are recognized
     at EVAL time, not read time — the reader produces App (Var name, ...)
     and eval dispatches on the head. is_complete correctly tracks
     line-comment completeness at EOF (it1 fix). The `\\` escape handler
     in read_string is correct as of it13 (was a stale advance-return bug;
     see BORGE-1783431485). pp_value round-trips VPair chains as proper
     lists and improper tails with `.` notation.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The reader parses Lisp surface syntax to expr
         values. Standard s-expressions: (foo bar baz),
         'symbol, "string", 123, #t, #f, ().

         Surface syntax is just an editor concern: a
         function stored in Postgres is its AST (JSONB),
         and the s-expr syntax is one of several possible
         editors (along with the htmx tree-editor that
         edits the AST directly).

         Special forms recognized at read or eval time
         (decision: eval time, the reader is generic):
           (lambda (args) body)
           (let ((name val)...) body)
           (letrec ((name val)...) body)
           (if test then else)
           (define name expr)        (* top-level binding *)
           (quote x)
           (deftype name (fields ...))
           (fork parent child)
           (merge source target)
           (app "prim-name" args)    (* explicit prim call *)

         Comments: ; to end of line. Docstrings: a leading
         string in a body position is the function's doc.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection eval
   (doc "The evaluator: env * expr -> value")
   (implements lisp/lib/env.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7: corrected stale it12 note.

    SHIPPED:
    - `Eval.eval : env -> expr -> value Lwt.t` (it12 Lwt-ABI).
    - Pure prims via register_sync; DB prims return promises.
    - Special forms: lambda/let/letrec/if/define/quote/seq/prim/
      with-transaction.
    - Per-call step budget IS implemented in lisp/lib/eval.ml:
      type budget { mutable steps; limit }, default_limit = 100_000,
      tick at every recursive eval step, raises Budget_exceeded.
      Earlier agent notes claiming "NO step budget" were stale.
    - 30 alcotest cases in lisp/test/eval_test.ml.

    STILL PARTIAL:
    (1) Quote of compounds: quote still accepts only Lit/Var;
        (quote (a b)) and nested App forms raise User_error
        "can only quote literals/symbols" (BORGE-1783383905).
        Spec intent is full s-expr quote; this blocks quoted data
        as first-class values.
    (2) Memory budget tracking (multi-user.budgets layer) deferred.
    (3) Explicit per-subcall budget raise + audit logging deferred.

    Status stays partial primarily for quote semantics + memory
    budget, NOT because step budgeting is missing.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The evaluator is PURE. It takes an env and an expr,
         returns a value or raises an interpreter error.
         No I/O in the interpreter. All side effects go
         through Prim calls, which the IMAGE layer wires to
         actual Postgres/SSH/HTTP side-effects.

         Cooperative evaluation: the eval loop is stepped;
         each Prim invocation yields back to the image's
         event loop. This gives per-session budgeting:
         - A step counter per call, configurable per session
         - When exceeded, that ONE call aborts
         - The session continues
         This prevents "user's bad function wedges the
         image" entirely.

         (define name expr) at top-level adds binding to
         current session's env AND persists to store. Edit
         = redefine (update the row). No versioning at the
         binding level beyond what branches provide.

         Application: evaluate operator + args, apply.
         Primitives are dispatched by name through a table
         the IMAGE layer registers at boot. The interpreter
         knows nothing about Postgres or htmx.

         Special forms handled in eval, not reader: lambda,
         let, letrec, if, define, quote. The reader just
         produces App/Variants of these as plain AST.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection primitives
   (doc "The ~20 impure functions the interpreter calls into")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7: demoted implemented -> partial.
    Primitive DISPATCH + a useful core set are shipped, but the section's
    own details still enumerate missing/stub prims, so `implemented` was
    an overclaim.

    v1 SHIPPED (image/lib/primitives.ml + crud.ml + session.ml):
    pure math (+ - * = < > <= >= not), list/pair (list car cdr cons
    null? pair? count), string (string-append str), reflection (type-of),
    session-aware (whoami current-author list-bindings), CRUD (get create
    update update-field delete rows select-for-update deftype), dict-over-
    JSON (dict-get dict-set dict-keys), branching (fork merge checkout),
    htmx (route render-html), with-transaction special form + private
    __with_transaction__ prim. All on Lwt-typed ABI (it12). 48 alcotest
    cases + multiple smoke tests.

    STUB / MISSING (keep section partial until these land or are
    explicitly removed from the doctrinal table):
    (1) (query 'type where: ...) — only (rows 'type [limit])
    (2) (describe 'type) / (inspect binding)
    (3) (diff source target)
    (4) (current-branch)
    (5) (list-branches) — registered but returns VNil stub
    (6) (hx-get)/(hx-post) — hx-get is a weak string-tag stub; hx-post
        missing; authors currently embed Hx attrs inside render-html
    (7) create returns VStr uuid, not VRef as details text says
    (8) author-scoped data filtering on CRUD (permissions gap)

    image.primitives-layer demoted in lockstep.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Primitives live in image/lib/primitives.ml. They
         are REAL OCaml, typed at the boundary, fast.
         Anything that touches Postgres, htmx, or the OS
         is a primitive, never user-Lisp.

         CRUD:
           (create 'type {fields...}) -> VRef
           (get 'type id) -> record value
           (update 'type id {fields...}) -> VRef
           (delete 'type id) -> VNil
           (query 'type where: ...) -> VList of records
           (select-for-update 'type id) -> record (locked)

         Branching:
           (fork parent child-name) -> branch-id
           (merge source target) -> status
           (current-branch) -> branch-id
           (checkout branch-id) -> VNil (* session switches *)
           (list-branches) -> VList
           (diff source target) -> VList of changes

         htmx/Routes:
           (route path fn)            (* register route *)
           (render-html node)          (* pure-html node *)
           (hx-get url) (hx-post url) (* attribute helpers *)

         Reflection:
           (type-of value) -> type-tag
           (describe 'type) -> schema
           (inspect binding) -> AST + metadata
           (list-bindings) -> VList

         Sessions:
           (whoami) -> username
           (current-author) -> username (* for perm checks *)
           (call-fn name args...)       (* resolves+calls *)

         All primitives that touch data consult the
         CURRENT-AUTHOR (from the binding being executed)
         for permission filtering, per multi-user model.

         Effect tags for each prim are defined in
         lisp-interpreter.effects (capability lattice).
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection effects
   (doc "Capability effects for Lisp programs: classify pure vs DB vs AI vs publish; infer; gate; display. Separate from OCaml 5 host effects and from Lwt async plumbing.")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (|it33 design sketch (roerick asked for effects + async
     clarity). IMPORTANT SPLIT:
       (1) ASYNC composition is ALREADY SOLVED at runtime: Eval.eval
           returns value Lwt.t; DB/AI prims return promises; pure prims
           use register_sync. (define u (get 'note id)) composes.
       (2) EFFECTS as policy/visibility are NOT solved: the language
           does not yet label or enforce Pure/ReadDb/WriteDb/Ai/Publish.
       (3) OCaml 5 algebraic effects are a possible FUTURE host runtime
           for the interpreter, NOT a substitute for Lisp-level
           capability effects. Do not migrate off Lwt for this feature.
     This subsection is the source-of-truth design; implement later as
     prim effect table + AST inference + UI badges + AI/route gates.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp image")
    (test "effect inference pure vs db vs ai on sample ASTs")
    (smoke "/bindings shows effect badges; AI propose rejects disallowed effects when policy on"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         0. THREE PROBLEMS PEOPLE CONFUSE (read this first)
         ==========================================================

         A) ASYNC / COMPOSITION  (mostly DONE)
            Can a function call (get ...) and use the result?
            YES today:
              (define u (get 'note id))
              (dict-get u "title")
            Mechanism: eval : env -> expr -> value Lwt.t
            Pure prims: register_sync wraps Lwt.return
            DB/AI prims: return promises directly
            This is the "async problem" for data-in-Lisp.
            It is runtime plumbing, not an effect system.

         B) CAPABILITY EFFECTS  (NOT DONE — this section)
            What powers does this function need?
            Pure calc vs read DB vs write DB vs AI vs publish page.
            Needed for: UI badges, AI safety, page policy,
            testing without services, author-runs-as-author story.

         C) HOST ALGEBRAIC EFFECTS (OPTIONAL LATER)
            OCaml 5 Effect.perform / handlers inside the
            interpreter implementation. Could one day replace
            some Lwt coloring. Does NOT give Lisp programs
            effects for free. Guest language still needs B.

         Do not block B on C. Do not re-solve A while building B.

         ==========================================================
         1. WHY EFFECTS
         ==========================================================

         Today every lambda looks the same:
           (lambda (x) (* x x))              ; pure
           (lambda () (get 'note id))        ; read DB
           (lambda () (create 'note "{}"))   ; write DB
           (lambda () (propose-function ...)); AI + write code
           (lambda () (render-html "..."))   ; page-ish

         The OS cares which is which. Effects make that
         first-class, inspectable, enforceable.

         ==========================================================
         2. EFFECT LATTICE (capability sets)
         ==========================================================

         Base labels (finite, small):

           Pure      no capability prims; only pure computation
           ReadDb    get, rows, select-for-update, list-branches, ...
           WriteDb   create, update, update-field, delete, deftype, ...
           Branch    fork, merge, checkout
           Publish   route (and later define-page helpers)
           Ai        propose-function / LLM client
           Html      render-html (soft; display intent, not security)
           Txn       body expects with-transaction scope
           Meta      define-persist side channel? (see note)

         A function's effect set is a SET of labels, e.g.
           {ReadDb, Html}
           {WriteDb, Ai}
           {}

         Ordering for policy (allowlist checks):
           Pure ⊂ anything
           ReadDb ⊂ ReadDb∪WriteDb  (writes imply reads usually)
           optional sugar: Db = ReadDb∪WriteDb

         NOTE on (define ...):
           Top-level define persists code. That is a Meta/code-store
           write performed by the REPL/session layer, not always an
           effect inside the defined body. Distinguish:
             - effects OF a function body (what it does when called)
             - effects OF defining it (session install + persist)

         ==========================================================
         3. PRIM EFFECT TABLE (initial map)
         ==========================================================

           Pure:
             + - * = < > <= >= not
             list car cdr cons null? pair? count
             string-append str type-of
             dict-get dict-set dict-keys
             hx-get hx-post          ; string tags only

           ReadDb:
             get rows select-for-update
             list-branches current-branch list-bindings
             whoami current-author   ; session read; treat ReadDb or soft Ambient

           WriteDb:
             create update update-field delete deftype

           Branch:
             fork merge checkout

           Publish:
             route

           Html:
             render-html

           Ai:
             propose-function
             (Ai usually pairs with WriteDb on code store via define path)

           Txn-related:
             with-transaction (special form) introduces Txn scope
             select-for-update meaningful only under Txn

         Image layer owns the table (primitives live in image/).
         Lisp package may define the effect datatype only.

         ==========================================================
         4. INFERENCE (syntactic, conservative)
         ==========================================================

         effects(expr) =
           union over subexpressions
           + effects of any prim head used in App
           + effects of known global bindings referenced
             (lookup function_defs metadata / env annotation)

         Rules of thumb:
           - overapproximate: if unsure, add effect
           - higher-order unknown fn => Effects.top or {ReadDb,WriteDb,Ai,Publish,Branch} policy knob
           - lambda body effects = function effects
           - quote bodies contribute no runtime effects

         On successful (define name body):
           store inferred effect set next to function
           (new column function_defs.effects jsonb OR side table OR
            derive on the fly from body AST — prefer derive-on-read
            for v1 to avoid migration, cache later)

         v1 implementation preference:
           derive-on-read from AST + prim table (no schema change)
           optional cache field later

         ==========================================================
         5. GATES / POLICY (where effects pay rent)
         ==========================================================

         a) UI badges
            /bindings and editor show: pure | db-read | db-write | ai | page

         b) AI propose policy (default)
            allowed = {Pure, ReadDb, Html}
            denied  = {WriteDb, Branch, Publish, Ai} unless user elevates
            (tune experimentally; document in product-experience)

         c) Route publish policy (optional v1)
            (route path fn) warns or rejects if fn has Branch/Ai
            pages should be ReadDb/Html/Pure by default

         d) Sandbox eval (future)
            browser anonymous do-it: Pure only

         e) Transactions
            if body has select-for-update or multi WriteDb,
            linter suggests with-transaction

         ==========================================================
         6. ASYNC STORY (what is already true)
         ==========================================================

         Solved:
           - eval returns value Lwt.t
           - prim_fn = value list -> value Lwt.t
           - composition of DB reads in Lisp works
           - with-transaction threads dynamic connection

         Not the same as effects:
           Lwt answers "when / how concurrency is scheduled"
           Effects answer "what capabilities are required"

         Example:
           (get 'note id) is BOTH
             async (returns promise through eval)
             AND ReadDb (capability)

         You already need both; only async exists today.

         ==========================================================
         7. RELATION TO OCAML 5 EFFECTS
         ==========================================================

         OCaml 5 algebraic effects MAY later implement host-side
         intercept for DB/AI inside the interpreter (handlers).
         That is a runtime rewrite of image/lisp internals.

         Explicit non-goals for first effects delivery:
           - do not replace Lwt in Mirage/httpaf/pgx stack
           - do not expose Effect.perform to Lisp users
           - do not require monadic syntax in Lisp

         Guest programs keep looking like normal Lisp.
         Effects are metadata + checks, not new call syntax
         (unless we add optional declare forms later).

         Optional sugar later:
           (define-pure name body)
           (define-page path body)
         Desugar to define + check inferred ⊆ declared.

         ==========================================================
         8. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase 0 (docs/UI copy): explain Pure vs DB vs AI in /about
         Phase 1: Effect module + prim -> effect set table
         Phase 2: infer_effects : expr -> EffectSet.t
         Phase 3: badges on /bindings + /edit + inspect
         Phase 4: AI propose allowlist gate (configurable)
         Phase 5: route publish warnings
         Phase 6 (later): session capability masks, setuid authority effect
         Phase 7 (optional research): OCaml effect handlers under eval

         ==========================================================
         9. DATA SHAPES (sketch)
         ==========================================================

         type effect =
           | Pure | ReadDb | WriteDb | Branch
           | Publish | Ai | Html | Txn

         type effect_set = effect list  (* unique sorted *)

         val prim_effects : string -> effect_set
         val infer : prim_table_meta -> expr -> effect_set
         val subset : effect_set -> effect_set -> bool
         val pp : effect_set -> string  (* "pure" / "read-db+html" *)

         No change to value or eval types required for Phases 1-5.

         ==========================================================
         10. SUCCESS CRITERIA
         ==========================================================

         - User can see whether a function is pure or touches DB/AI
         - AI-proposed functions can be policy-gated by effects
         - Pure functions remain testable without Postgres
         - Async composition remains intact (no regression of Lwt ABI)
         - Spec/docs stop conflating async with effects
    |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section storage
  (doc "Postgres schema: branches, function_defs, type_defs, sessions. SERIALIZABLE defaults. NO inheritance.")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|Iteration 30 (2026-07-06) plan-pass #6: section status
    lifted partial -> implemented. All four subsections are now
    `implemented`: schema (it2), no-inheritance (it9), transactions
    (SERIALIZABLE defaults it16 + select-for-update it17 + with-transaction
    it29 + dynamic-tx-scope it29). The ONE remaining v1.5 ergonomics
    addition is the opt-in weaker-isolation `(query ... isolation:
    'read-committed)` passthrough (the with-transaction prim already
    accepts the isolation arg shape, defaulting to SERIALIZABLE; the
    weaker-isolation passthrough is additive when shipped). This is not
    a v1 necessity; remember per-subsection details below for the
    fine-grained status narrative.|) *)
  (subsection schema
   (doc "Core tables (in migrations/001_branches.sql)")
   (implements image/lib/store.ml supervisor/lib/store.ml auth/lib/store.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Schema lives in migrations/001_branches.sql (branches,
     function_defs, type_defs, view_bindings, sessions,
     schema_migrations) and migrations/003_data.sql (the shared
     `data_rows` table for user-declared types). All SQL
     applied cleanly by the supervisor's migration runner
     (which owns `split_sql_statements` to work around pgx's
     multi-statement prepared-statement protocol limitation —
     see BORGE-1783527733). SERIALIZABLE-by-default lives in
     image/lib/store.ml's `Store.with_transaction`. Verified
     end-to-end against the temp PG 18.4 cluster at /tmp/pp-pgsup:
     11 tables created, migrations idempotent on re-run.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         -- The shared code store
         CREATE TABLE branches (
           id          uuid PRIMARY KEY,
           parent_id   uuid REFERENCES branches(id),
           owner       text NOT NULL,
           name        text NOT NULL,
           created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
           last_synced timestamptz,  -- for merge diffing
           UNIQUE (owner, name)
         );

         CREATE TABLE function_defs (
           branch_id   uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES branches(id)
                          ON DELETE CASCADE,
           name        text NOT NULL,
           body        jsonb NOT NULL,  -- serialized Lisp AST
           doc         text,
           author      text NOT NULL,
           updated_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
           PRIMARY KEY (branch_id, name)
         );

         CREATE TABLE type_defs (
           branch_id   uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES branches(id)
                          ON DELETE CASCADE,
           name        text NOT NULL,
           fields      jsonb NOT NULL,  -- field declarations
           migrated    bool NOT NULL DEFAULT false,
           schema_vers  int NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
           author      text NOT NULL,
           updated_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
           PRIMARY KEY (branch_id, name)
         );

         CREATE TABLE view_bindings (
           branch_id   uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES branches(id)
                          ON DELETE CASCADE,
           route       text NOT NULL,  -- e.g. "/inspect/:id"
           handler     text NOT NULL,  -- function name
           PRIMARY KEY (branch_id, route)
         );

         CREATE TABLE sessions (
           id          uuid PRIMARY KEY,
           user_id     text NOT NULL,
           branch_id   uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES branches(id),
           pid         int,           -- image instance pid
           boot_mode   text NOT NULL,  -- 'on-connect'|'always'
           health      text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'unknown',
           started_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
           last_seen   timestamptz
         );

         -- Shared data tables (event, user, etc.) are
         -- created by migrations emitted from type_defs
         -- merged to main. Their shape is whatever main
         -- says. They are NOT branched.

         CREATE TABLE schema_migrations (
           version     int PRIMARY KEY,
           applied_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
           description text
         );

         The data tables themselves (e.g. events, users)
         live alongside these system tables, created by the
         supervisor's migration runner when type_defs are
         accepted into main.

         SERIALIZABLE is the transaction isolation default
         for ALL Lisp-driven mutations. Weaker isolation is
         opt-in per query.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (build "psql -f migrations/001_branches.sql + 002_auth.sql + 003_data.sql = 11 tables")
    (smoke "supervisor migration runner applies 3 migrations idempotently (supervisor smoke in it4/it5 logs)"))
  )
  (subsection no-inheritance
   (doc "Why we do NOT use Postgres INHERITS")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Confirmed in code (it1-it16): no INHERITS in any
     migration; plain tables with `branch_id` FK + PRIMARY KEY
     (branch_id, name). The override semantics the spec describes
     is realized via a recursive-CTE walk up the parent chain
     (closest-ancestor-wins), used in `Store.lookup_function`,
     `Store.list_function_names`, `Store.list_routes`,
     `Store.list_types`, and the CRUD data-row lookups. Override-vs-
     union behavior verified end-to-end by /tmp/pp_override_it9.py:
     alice/experiment overrides `greet`; system/main's `greet` is
     unchanged (branch isolation), alice's session sees alice's
     version (override wins).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Postgres table inheritance (INHERITS) is the
         deprecated feature from the early-90s Postgres
         implementation. We deliberately do NOT use it.

         THE CORE REASON: branches are an OVERRIDE
         primitive, not a UNION primitive. When Alice
         forks and overrides 'greet', her branch's view
         should see ONLY her greet, not main's greet
         merged in. Postgres inheritance does union
         (both rows visible), not override.

         Modeling override on top of inheritance forces
         us to write override resolution ourselves anyway
         (pick the child's row when it exists), so
         inheritance buys nothing but its quirks.

         Other reasons:
         - Foreign keys / unique constraints don't span
           inheritance trees properly
         - PRIMARY KEY doesn't propagate
         - The planner handles inherited queries with edge
           cases and unpredictability
         - Inheritance handles schema evolution (add/remove
           columns) inconsistently; migrations would be a
           horror show
         - pg_dump, psql, admin tools render inheritance
           weirdly
         - We'd pin ourselves to non-standard semantics

         Plain tables with branch_id FK and override
         resolution in queries (recursive CTE up the
         parent chain, or materialized effective-definition)
         gives us:
         - Clean indexed SELECTs
         - Predictable planner behavior
         - Standard migrations
         - Full inspectability (the goal)
         - A clean path to content-addressing later if we
           want "actually git" semantics

         Future options stay open. Inheritance forecloses
         them.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_override_it9.py — override-walk + branch isolation (closest-ancestor wins, parent unchanged)"))
  )
  (subsection transactions
   (doc "Transaction isolation: SERIALIZABLE by default")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Iteration 30 (2026-07-06) plan-pass #6: status lifted
     partial -> implemented. All v1 sub-features of this section are now
     built and smoke-tested: SERIALIZABLE-by-default (it16), explicit
     row locks via `(select-for-update 'type id)` (it17), and dynamic
     transaction scoping via `(with-transaction (lambda () body))` (it29)
     which gives select-for-update real cross-statement locking scope.
     Rollback behavior verified end-to-end on live PG.

     The ONE remaining unbuilt sub-feature is the opt-in weaker-isolation
     `(query ... isolation: 'read-committed)` passthrough — a v1.5
     ergonomics addition, not a v1 necessity. The `with-transaction` prim
     already accepts the `isolation` arg shape (defaults to SERIALIZABLE);
     lifting the v1.5 passthrough is purely additive when shipped.

     Cache coherence (initial, NO caching — each call reads fresh from
     PG) is the current reality and is implemented as documented.
     LISTEN/NOTIFY-based invalidation is still deferred (later).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         All Lisp-driven mutations run in SERIALIZABLE
         transactions by default. Postgres detects
         conflicts at commit and aborts one, forcing
         retry. For a multi-user personal OS, throughput
         is not the concern; correctness is.

         Opt-in weaker isolation:
           (query ... isolation: 'read-committed)
         Available but discouraged. Most user code never
         touches this; the system does the safe thing by
         default. (v1.5 — the with-transaction prim
         already accepts the isolation arg shape,
         defaulting to SERIALIZABLE; the weaker-isolation
         passthrough remains additive when shipped.)

         Explicit row locks:
           (select-for-update 'type id)
         Exposed as a primitive for finer control.
         Persists across subsequent prims in the same
         with-transaction body (it29).

         Cache coherence (initial): NO caching of stored
         function defs in image memory. Each call reads
         the AST fresh from Postgres. For a personal OS
         where most calls are query+render, the function
         read alongside the query is acceptable overhead.

         Cache coherence (later): LISTEN/NOTIFY-based
         invalidation. When a function is updated, the
         updating image notifies; others drop from cache.
         Mechanism well-trodden, deferred until measured.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — 48 alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "duplicate-fork UNIQUE violation triggers pgx auto-rollback (it16)")
    (smoke "fork_branch returns new branch id (str round-trip)")
    (smoke "merge_branch returns merged-rows count")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_sfu_it17.py — (select-for-update 'type id) prim"))
  )
  (subsection with-transaction
   (doc "Dynamic transaction scoping: (with-transaction (lambda () body))")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Iteration 29 (2026-07-06): Option A from BORGE-1783438027
     landed. Adds session-level dynamic transaction binding via a mutable
     `current_tx : Pg.t option` field on `Store.t`; prims use
     `Store.current_conn t` so they see the in-tx conn automatically.
     New `with_transaction` special form in lisp/eval.ml dispatches the
     lambda body through a privately-named `__with_transaction__` prim
     registered from session.ml (keeps lisp/ pure-I/O-free). Nested
     transactions raise a clean Interp_error. Body value passes through
     verbatim. Borge issue BORGE-1783438027 status: triage -> resolved.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Dynamic transaction scope: all DB prims called inside the
         body run against the SAME pgx transactional connection.
         Defaults to SERIALIZABLE isolation.

           (with-transaction (lambda ()
             (seq (select-for-update 'type id)
                  (update-field 'type id "field" "value"))))

         The select-for-update row lock PERSISTS across the
         subsequent update-field in the same body — this is the
         v1 concurrency-protection story for compound mutations.

         Nested transactions raise a clean Interp_error. The body
         value is returned verbatim (no wrapper tags/boxing).

         The Lambda body is evaluated lazily; the dynamic binding
         is set on `Store.t.current_tx` before the body runs and
         cleared via `Lwt.finalize` after (whether BODY succeeds or
         raises — pgx still sees the propagated exception and rolls
         back).
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — 48 alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_tx_it29.py — with-transaction prim (7 assertions)"))
  )
 )
 (section multi-user
  (doc "Per-user image instances, namespaces via branch ownership, budgets, author-runs-as-author")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7: section overall partial.
    isolation = implemented; namespaces = partial (no cross-branch
    qualified calls); budgets = partial (step budget SHIPPED in eval,
    memory/raise-budget deferred — earlier notes wrongly said planned/
    missing); permissions = partial (author column + admin merge yes,
    runtime setuid + row scoping no). User-facing multi-user model is
    in place at process/branch level; finer permission semantics remain.|) *)
  (subsection isolation
   (doc "Process-level isolation: one image per user")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it6 / verified it6 onward: the
     supervisor's `Fleet.boot` (supervisor/lib/fleet.ml) spawns a
     per-user image (unix process) on SSH connect and sets
     PP_USER_ID/USERNAME/BRANCH_ID/BRANCH_NAME env vars so each
     image boots as the verified user. Auth is the separate authd
     unikernel (auth/) that verifies the SSH key via the auth RPC;
     the supervisor never sees raw credentials, just gets back the
     user_id or denial. The image trusts the supervisor's identity
     claim via PP_USER_ID (the internal network is the trust
     boundary). End-to-end verified by /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py: admin's
     SSH key → auth verifies → supervisor spawns image inst-001 →
     REPL proxy → (define square (lambda (x) (* x x))) → (square 7)
     -> 49. An infinite loop OR bad function in alice's image takes
     only alice's image offline; the supervisor's other connections
     (admin, other users) are unaffected (each is its own Lwt.async
     per supervisor/lib/ssh.ml's per-connection dispatch).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Multi-user is achieved by giving each user their
         OWN image unikernel, not by namespacing within one
         shared image. This is the Unix/POSIX model:
         - Each user has a "home" (their branch bindings)
         - Logging in = booting your image
         - Each image is a separate process

         Wins:
         - Alice's infinite loop wedges Alice's image only
         - Alice's bad function definition takes only her
           image offline; she recovers by rolling back to
           her last-good branch
         - The interpreter doesn't need cooperative
           scheduling for cross-user safety (only for
           in-session budgeting)
         - Per-user A/B rollback (finer than whole-image)

         Costs:
         - N images always running if "always-on" pinned
           (RAM committed)
         - Boot-on-connect default mitigates this: idle
           users' images aren't running

         Auth: a SEPARATE auth unikernel (see section
         auth) verifies credentials and resolves user
         identities. The supervisor never sees raw
         credentials — it passes them to auth and gets
         back a user_id (or denial). The image trusts the
         supervisor's identity claim because: the
         supervisor asserted it AND the supervisor got it
         from auth, AND image/supervisor are on the
         internal network. The image itself does no
         credential verification.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection namespaces
   (doc "No privileged 'common'; branches are namespaces")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|Implemented: there is no privileged 'common'
     namespace. A binding's key is (branch_id, name) per the
     migrations/001_branches.sql schema; when alice forks system/main
     to alice/main she gets her own function_defs row for `greet`
     independent of main's `greet` until she merges. Admin-only merges
     to system branches ARE enforced (per policy at image/lib/
     session.ml's `merge` prim and branching.merge subsection).
     Verified by /tmp/pp_override_it9.py (alice/experiment fork of
     system/main sees inherited `greet` via recursive-CTE override
     walk; alice overrides; alice's session sees alice's; system/main
     unchanged).

     NOT YET IMPLEMENTED — cross-branch calls: a function can NOT
     yet explicitly reference another branch's binding via a qualified
     name (e.g. `(system/main:greet)`). All function calls consult the
     current branch's env only (with override-walk up the parent chain).
     Cross-branch qualified references are a v1.5 ergonomic addition.

     Section marked `partial` until cross-branch qualified calls land.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         There is no privileged 'common' namespace. There
         is 'main' (system baseline) and per-user branches.
         Namespacing IS branch ownership.

         A binding's key is (branch_id, name). When Alice
         forks main to alice/main, she gets her own
         'greet' independent of main's greet (until she
         merges).

         Cross-branch calls: a function can explicitly
         reference another branch's binding via a qualified
         name. Normally a function calls bindings in its
         own branch.

         The 'common namespace problem' is thus reframed
         as the 'merge to main politics problem':
         - Default: admin-only merges to main
         - All users can fork freely
         - Users can merge into their own branches freely
         - Politics expand later (PR/review workflow)

         This sidesteps the up-or-down political decision
         the original 'publish to common' model forced.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection budgets
   (doc "Per-call step budgets to bound runaway user code")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7: corrected stale it23 note.

    SHIPPED:
    (1) Process isolation — each user gets their own image process
        via Fleet.boot; a wedged image only takes that user offline.
    (2) Per-call evaluator step budget — lisp/lib/eval.ml ticks a
        budget at every recursive eval step; default_limit 100_000;
        raises Budget_exceeded for that call. Earlier notes claiming
        "step budget NOT shipped" were wrong after the budget code
        landed in eval.

    STILL DEFERRED (keeps section partial):
    - Memory-budget tracking per session/call
    - Explicit primitive to raise budget for a legitimate long
      sub-call, with audit logging
    - Configurable per-session default budget from manifest/config
    - Supervisor-side kill/restart automation when an image is
      wedged despite budgets (health auto-restart loop)

    Step budget + process isolation are the two load-bearing layers
    for runaway-code defense today.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "scripts/dev.sh start + ssh admin@host -p 12223 boots an isolated image process"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Each Lisp primitive invocation runs with a step
         counter. When exceeded, that ONE call aborts;
         the session continues. This prevents "one user's
         bad function wedges the whole image" at the
         interpreter level (defense in depth alongside
         process-level isolation).

         Default budget: configurable per session, sensible
         initial value (e.g. 100k eval steps). User can
         request more for legitimate long-running operations
         via a primitive that explicitly raises the budget
         for a sub-call (and logs it).

         The interpreter checks the counter at every Prim
         invocation and every recursive eval depth. Cheap:
         one integer increment per step.

         Memory: also tracked. A session exceeding memory
         budget aborts the offending call. (Deferred; step
         budget is the v1 mechanism.)

         This is per-CALL, not per-session: a session can
         make many calls, each independently bounded.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection permissions
   (doc "Author-runs-as-author: functions execute with their author's data perms")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|Partially implemented as of it19 plan-pass #3.

     IMPLEMENTED (the OCaml-side governance half):
     - Admin-only merges to system branches IS enforced (it10; see
       branching.merge) — this is half of the "admin owns main /
       merges require admin approval" permissions story.
     - function_defs and type_defs carry an `author` text column,
       populated from manifest.username at (define ...) / (deftype ...)
       / (route ...) time. Store.lookup_function + list_function_names
       return author alongside the body; it's surfaced in the
       /binding/<name> system page UI.
     - `(current-author)` prim is registered (it7) returning the
       session user — the FIRST HALF of the spec's author-runs-as-
       author pattern (reading the author for visibility).

     NOT YET IMPLEMENTED (the runtime-permission half):
     - `(current-author)` is the SESSION user, NOT "the executed
       binding's author" — the interpreter does NOT thread the
       AUTHOR of the currently-executing binding through eval.
       So a function authored by alice and called by bob runs as
       bob (the session user), NOT as alice. This is a meaningful
       security gap from the spec's setuid model: alice-authoring a
       function that reads her calendar and bob calling it would
       read bob's calendar today (or fail), not alice's.
     - Data prims (get/create/update/delete/rows) do NOT consult
       current-author for permission filtering — they operate on
       the shared `data_rows` table without per-author row scoping.
       In v1 every image sees every row in its data_rows-table shape;
       isolation depends on per-user PG roles or per-user tables,
       which isn't wired yet (data_rows is schema-shared).

     Section marked `partial` until the runtime-author-threading +
     per-author row scoping lands. v1 ships the SECURITY MODEL
     halfway: governance policy (admin-only merges) yes; runtime
     setuid semantics no. Documented honestly so a perusing user
     knows what NOT to assume about a privileged function's blast
     radius today.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         A function executes with ITS AUTHOR's data
         permissions, not the caller's. This is the Unix
         setuid model: if alice authors a function that
         reads her calendar, and bob calls it, the function
         reads alice's calendar (not bob's), and bob sees
         only what alice's function returns.

         Implications:
         - The interpreter's eval carries a (current-author)
           field, read from the EXECUTED binding's author,
           not the session user.
         - Data primitives (query, get, create, etc.)
           consult current-author for permission filtering.
         - A careless privileged function is a security
           hole, same as Unix setuid. Honest and familiar.
         - Functions can expose capabilities to other
           users by name without exposing raw rows.

         Authoring permissions (separate concern):
         - A binding in alice/main is callable by anyone
           in the same image (read access implicit).
         - Only alice can edit bindings in alice/* branches.
         - main is owned by admin; merges require admin
           approval (default).

         This model is the Unix model, by choice. It maps
         cleanly to "SSH-first, multi-user OS" framing. If
         we wanted a Lisp-machine feel (shared trusted
         environment), we'd pick differently, but
         SSH-first implies Unix-style multi-user.
   |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section auth
  (doc "A separate auth unikernel: credential verification, identity resolution, session tokens")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7: section remains partial. Shipped:
    credentials-store, bootstrap-admin (first admin via env), audit-log,
    verification ssh_key + token mint/verify halves, rpc-from-supervisor
    plain-HTTP half. NEW planned product subsections this pass:
    login-experience, web-login, user-management — these were previously
    only implied inside verification/bootstrap details and never owned.
    Password verify still Denied stub (argon2 not on poohstack).
    admin_api create_user/add_credential still TODO stubs.|) *)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Auth is its own unikernel, separate from the
        supervisor. This is deliberate, not just
        componentization:

        - AUDIT/TRUST BOUNDARY: auth holds credentials
          and verifies them. If compromised, the whole
          system is compromised. This image should be
          small, boring, never touched. The supervisor
          changes often; auth changes rarely. Separating
          means supervisor changes never risk auth code.
        - BLAST RADIUS: a memory bug in the supervisor
          cannot reach into auth's address space to read
          credentials. (Both are unikernels with their
          own address spaces; no shared memory.)
        - RESTART INDEPENDENCE: supervisor restarts
          (new fleet logic shipped) don't kill active
          sessions (auth keeps running, tokens stay
          valid). Auth restarts (rare) are explicit.
        - DEPENDENCY HYGIENE: auth pulls crypto
          primitives (argon2, ed25519, HMAC). Supervisor
          pulls process management. Each unikernel's
          dep surface stays small and Mirage-idiomatic.

        Auth's job is FOUR PURE FUNCTIONS of the
        credential store (in Postgres):
          verify(cred) -> May<user_id>
          lookup(user_id) -> May<User>
          mint(user_id) -> token
          verify_token(token) -> May<user_id>
        Plus boot/attach provisioning, which is the
        supervisor's job, NOT auth's. Auth knows
        nothing about the fleet.

        Auth is the ONLY thing that holds the session
        token signing key. The supervisor never sees
        raw credentials (passwords, keys) past the
        initial verify call — it only sees user_ids and
        signed tokens. Good separation.
   |))
  )
  (subsection credentials-store
   (doc "The users, credentials, sessions, auth_log tables in Postgres")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|All tables shipped in migrations/002_auth.sql: users,
     credentials, auth_sessions [renamed from sessions to avoid the
     name clash with the image's sessions table, see BORGE-1783389549
     note], auth_log, system_keys. The `pricklypear_auth` PG role is
     GRANTed SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE ONLY on auth tables (not the
     branches/function_defs/data_rows tables) — defense-in-depth role
     isolation as the spec describes. auth/lib/store.ml connects as
     `pricklypear_auth`. All id columns have DEFAULT
     gen_random_uuid() (it5 migration fix). auth_log accumulates
     ssh_key verify rows verified end-to-end through /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py.

     HARDENING TODO carried forward (documented in spec text below):
     move the session_signing key from system_keys (on-disk in PG) to
     auth-image MEMORY ONLY (env var on boot). v1 keeps the key in
     system_keys for restart survival; the spec calls this an
     accepted personal/small-team risk now.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Tables (added by migrations, see migrations):

           users (id uuid PK, username text UNIQUE,
                  is_admin bool, created_at timestamptz)

           credentials (id uuid PK, user_id uuid FK,
                        kind text,        -- 'ssh_key'|'password'|'web_token'|'api_key'
                        public_data jsonb,  -- SSH key material for verification;
                                            -- null for password (hash is secret)
                        secret_data jsonb,   -- argon2 hash for password;
                                            -- reference for api_key
                        created_at timestamptz,
                        last_used_at timestamptz)

           sessions (id uuid PK,            -- the token's opaque id
                     user_id uuid FK,
                     token_hash text,      -- HMAC of token; lookup target
                     expires_at timestamptz,
                     created_at timestamptz,
                     revoked_at timestamptz NULL)

           auth_log (id uuid PK, user_id text NULL,
                     kind text, success bool,
                     source_ip text,
                     happened_at timestamptz)

           system_keys (id text PK,        -- 'session_signing'
                        key_data bytea,    -- the HMAC signing key, raw
                        created_at timestamptz)

         KEY STORAGE (
          in-Postgres, role-isolated):
         - The session signing key lives in
           system_keys, read on auth boot.
         - system_keys is in a dedicated PG role
           that ONLY the auth image connects as.
           A DB dump from any other role does not
           include it (defense-in-depth, free).
         - KNOWN RISK: a DB dump from auth's role,
           or an auth-image compromise, leaks the
           signing key -> all web sessions forgeable.
           Accepted for now (personal/small-team OS).
         - HARDENING GOAL (when this becomes stable &
           useful): move the signing key to
           auth-image MEMORY ONLY (env var on boot,
           never touches disk). Tokens survive auth
           restart only if the same key re-supplied.
           Invalidating all sessions on key rotation
           is acceptable then.
         - Other credentials (password hashes via
           argon2id, SSH public keys) in Postgres
           permanently; that's correct and stable
           (these are public verification material,
           not signing keys).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection verification
   (doc "verify(cred) / lookup / mint / verify_token — the four pure functions")
   (implements auth/lib/verify.ml auth/lib/tokens.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it5/verified it6 onward for the
     SSH-key path; token path verified by auth/test/auth_test.ml (4
     alcotest cases for HMAC determinism, random-opaque_id 32B,
     round-trip, malformed-token rejection).

     IMPLEMENTED:
     - `verify_ssh_key(presented_key) -> May<user_id>` full path:
       auth/lib/verify.ml + auth/lib/store.ml SELECT the
       credential WHERE kind='ssh_key' AND public_data=jsonb;
       returns Ok uid or Denied. End-to-end verified: denied key →
       {"ok":false}; admin key → {"ok":true,"user_id":"<uuid>"}.
     - `mint(user_id)` + `verify_token(token)` via auth/lib/tokens.ml:
       HMAC-SHA256 with the system_keys signing key; opaque_id is
       32 random bytes; token format is base64(opaque_id || sig);
       verify recomputes HMAC + compares. Mirage_crypto_rng
       initialized via Mirage_crypto_rng_unix.initialize (module
       Fortuna) on authd boot.

     NOT YET IMPLEMENTED (deferred — v1.5 / requires an opam install):
     - `verify_password(username, password)`: STUB only — raises
       `Not_implemented` because argon2 is NOT on the poohstack opam
       switch. No web-login password verification. ACCEPTABLE for v1
       because the SSH-first model means the primary credential IS
       the SSH key; password verification is the future web-login
       path. v1.5 will install `argon2` opam package + wire a real
       argon2id verify.

     Section marked `partial` until at least verify_password lands
     (the four pure functions the spec enumerates include both
     ssh_key AND password cred kinds).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         SSH KEY VERIFICATION (for supervisor SSH front door):
         1. Supervisor does SSH handshake, receives
            the presented public key.
         2. Supervisor calls auth:
            verify_ssh_key(presented_key) -> May<user_id>
         3. Auth: parse key, SELECT FROM credentials
            WHERE kind='ssh_key' AND public_data = key.
            Return user_id or denied.
         4. Supervisor calls auth.lookup(user_id) ->
            User record (for is_admin, etc.).
         5. Auth logs to auth_log.

         PASSWORD VERIFICATION (for web login):
         1. User POSTs username+password to the
            supervisor's /login (or the auth image's
            own endpoint — TBD; either way).
         2. Auth: SELECT secret_data FROM credentials
            WHERE kind='password' AND user_id=
            (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username=$1).
            argon2-verify against the presented
            password. Return user_id or denied.
         3. Auth logs to auth_log.

         TOKEN MINTING (after web login):
         1. Auth: generate random token bytes (32B),
            HMAC-sign with the session_signing key
            from system_keys, opaque_id = random,
            signed = HMAC(opaque_id).
         2. Auth: INSERT INTO sessions (id=opaque_id,
            user_id, token_hash=signed, expires_at=...).
         3. Auth: return token = base64(opaque_id ||
            signed). Client stores as cookie.

         TOKEN VERIFICATION (on web requests):
         1. Client sends token cookie. Routing layer
            (supervisor or relayd-style proxy) calls
            auth.verify_token(token).
         2. Auth: split token, recompute HMAC,
            compare; if match, lookup sessions row,
            check not revoked and not expired. Return
            user_id or denied.
         3. Auth logs to auth_log.

         TOKEN FORMAT (minimal, NOT JWT):
         opaque_id (32B random) || HMAC(opaque_id)
         We control both ends; don't need JWT's
         cross-system interop claims. Hash stored in
         sessions table enables revocation.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection bootstrap-admin
   (doc "First-admin creation via env var on auth first boot")
   (implements auth/lib/admin_api.ml auth/lib/boot.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it5/verified it6 onward. auth/lib/
     boot.ml checks: if users table is empty AND PP_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_KEY
     env var is set, INSERT into users (username='admin',
     is_admin=true) + INSERT into credentials (kind='ssh_key',
     public_data=the key, user_id=admin). Verified end-to-end:
     /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py sends the SAME admin SSH key as authd was
     supplied via env var on first boot; the verify_ssh_key call
     returns the admin's user_id. (When users table is non-empty, the
     env var is ignored on subsequent boots — idempotent bootstrap.)
     Subsequent users are NOT part of this subsection's done bar —
     see auth.user-management (planned; admin_api still stubs).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Bootstrapping the first user, with no users yet:

         1. Operator sets env var on the auth unikernel
            at first boot:
              PRICKLYPEAR_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_KEY=<ssh-ed25519 AAAA...>
         2. Auth boot: if users table is empty AND the
            env var is set, INSERT into users
            (username='admin', is_admin=true) and into
            credentials (kind='ssh_key',
            public_data=the key, user_id=admin).
         3. Auth boot: warn loudly in logs that
            bootstrap was used, and recommend clearing
            the env var afterward.
         4. After first boot, the env var is ignored.

         Subsequent users: added by an admin via the
         supervisor's admin UI (which itself authenticates
         via auth) calling an auth primitive
         (create-user username, add-credential user_id
         ssh_key, etc.). These primitives require
         is_admin=true on the calling session.

         No interactive bootstrap flow needed; the env-var
         pattern is simple, standard, and audit-logged.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection audit-log
   (doc "Every verification logged to auth_log")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it5: auth/lib/store.ml's `audit_log`
     INSERTs a row (user_id, kind, success, source_ip=NULL,
     happened_at default now()) per verify_ssh_key call. Verified:
     `/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py` succession of one successful admin verify
     + one denied verify leave behind two auth_log rows
     (kind='ssh_key', success=t then f). Use cases from the spec text
     below — synthesized log for anomalies (cred misuse, brute-force,
     insider abuse) — are achievable with the existing log shape; a
     future stats page on the supervisor admin could surface these.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Every call to verify_ssh_key, password
         verification, mint, and verify_token writes a
         row to auth_log:
           (user_id NULL if unknown/failed,
            kind: 'ssh_key'|'password'|'mint'|'verify_token',
            success bool,
            source_ip text,
            happened_at timestamptz default now())

         WHY from day one (despite personal-OS scope):
         - Cheap to add (one INSERT per verify call).
         - Invaluable when something goes wrong
           (compromised key? malicious insider? audit
           compliance if ever needed?).
         - Painful to retrofit (verification call sites
           scattered; adding log calls everywhere later
           means finding every one).
         - Standard practice; non-controversial.

         The log is append-only in practice
         (no UPDATE/DELETE on auth_log). Retention
         policy deferred; for now, keep forever
         (cheap rows).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection rpc-from-supervisor
   (doc "How supervisor calls auth: internal signed/mTLS RPC")
   (implements supervisor/lib/auth_client.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it6/verified it6 onward. supervisor/
     lib/auth_client.ml does an HTTP POST to authd's /verify_ssh_key
     endpoint (hand-rolled HTTP/1.1 over plain Lwt_unix socket; reads
     response bytes until `}` or EOF; parses JSON; returns Ok uid or
     Denied). Fail-closed: any transport error logs + returns Denied.
     End-to-end verified by /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py through the dev.sh stack:
     python connects to supervisor SSH stub :17822 → supervisor calls
     authd → authd verifies → image spawned. The supervisor itself never
     holds raw credentials in memory past the initial in-flight RPC call.
     (See BORGE-1783389549 for the note that hand-rolled HTTP-over-socket
     was needed because Lwt_io channels triggered EBADF mixed with raw
     Lwt_unix.read/write on the same fd.)

     DEFERRED from spec text: the actual spec doctrinal text below
     describes a signed/mTLS RPC. v1 uses plain HTTP+JSON on the
     internal network (loopback in dev, the inter-unikernel fabric in
     prod). The internal network IS the trust boundary in v1: images,
     supervisor, and authd share an isolated fabric; spoofing the
     authd address requires being on the fabric.

     Section marked `partial` until (1) a real mTLS or signed-token RPC
     layer lands (a future hardening iteration that would let authd
     REJECT unauthenticated supervisor RPCs — currently the
     supervisor's identity is implicit via network position). Also
     deferred: token-result passing through the supervisor (v1 returns
     the user_id straight from the SSH verify RPC, no separate token
     minting on the SSH path because the SSH session IS the auth).TOKEN
     verification RPC IS implemented (authd /verify_token endpoint)
     for the future web-login path.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The supervisor calls auth over an internal RPC
         (HTTP+JSON, or a tighter binary protocol — HTTP
         is simpler and auth doesn't need throughput).
         On the internal hypervisor network.

         AuthENTICATION OF THE RPC ITSELF:
         - mTLS between supervisor and auth:
           both present certs signed by a shared CA
           (the CA is the hypervisor itself, or a
           generated root for the deployment).
         - OR simpler: a shared static secret in a
           header, both images load from env vars,
           rotated manually. Less secure but v1-OK
           for a trusted internal network.
         - Either way: auth refuses calls from anything
           that doesn't present the right credential.
           The supervisor is the only legitimate caller.

         Endpoints auth exposes (internal only):
           POST /verify_ssh_key   (key) -> {user_id or denied}
           POST /verify_password  (user, pw) -> {user_id or denied}
           POST /mint_token       (user_id) -> {token, expires_at}
           POST /verify_token     (token) -> {user_id or denied}
           GET  /lookup/:user_id             -> {user} (admin only)
           POST /admin/create_user (admin only)
           POST /admin/add_credential (admin only)

         The supervisor holds a long-lived client cert
         (or shared secret) for these calls. It is the
         only caller; auth refuses all others.

         v1 SHIPPED: plain HTTP+JSON on internal network,
         /verify_ssh_key and token paths live.
         DEFERRED: mTLS/shared-secret RPC auth of the
         caller; full admin endpoint wiring (see
         auth.user-management).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection login-experience
   (doc "What 'log in' means to a human: one front door into your live image")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it32: PARTIAL. scripts/repl.sh + repl_client.py give a
     human front door (key auth -> REPL) without netcat. Browser still
     requires an already-booted image. Full "I logged into my computer"
     browser path is auth.web-login.|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "human authenticates and lands in their live image REPL or browser session")
    (smoke "unknown credential is denied without booting an image"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PRODUCT BAR (v1 experience):
         1. User presents a credential (SSH public key by
            default; password/web token via auth.web-login).
         2. authd verifies; supervisor resolves identity.
         3. Supervisor boots or attaches the user's image
            (boot-on-connect default).
         4. User lands in a live environment: SSH/line
            REPL and/or browser session bound to that image.
         5. Session end tears down boot-on-connect images;
            store state survives.

         NON-GOALS for this subsection:
         - Implementing crypto (ssh-interface / awa path)
         - Password hashing details (verification)
         - Admin user CRUD (user-management)

         Success looks like: "I logged into my computer",
         not "I ran a smoke protocol against port 17822".
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection web-login
   (doc "Browser login: password verify, mint token, cookie, attach to image UI")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (|it32: still planned. Password verify + mint/verify_token
     now work (interim sha256$salt$digest, not argon2). Still missing:
     /login page, cookie wiring, supervisor attach/proxy to image UI.
     user-management create_user/add_credential password path ships.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build auth supervisor image")
    (smoke "POST login with valid password -> token cookie -> image UI session")
    (smoke "bad password denied + auth_log row"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Planned flow:
         1. User opens supervisor or image login page
         2. POST username + password
         3. authd verify_password (argon2id) -> user_id
         4. authd mint_token -> Set-Cookie
         5. Subsequent requests: verify_token -> attach
            or route to that user's image HTTP surface
         6. Logout revokes auth_sessions row

         v1 may keep login on supervisor and reverse-proxy
         to the image; exact host split is a make-time
         choice. SSH remains the primary admin/dev path
         until web-login is verified.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection user-management
   (doc "After bootstrap: create users, add credentials, list users (admin)")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|it32: IMPLEMENTED. admin_api create_user +
     add_credential ssh_key|password; HTTP
     /admin/create_user|/admin/add_credential|/admin/list_users with
     X-Admin header. Password hash interim sha256$salt$digest.
     Smoke in scripts/dream_product_smoke.py.|) *)
   (implements auth/lib/admin_api.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build auth")
    (smoke "admin creates user + adds ssh_key credential; new user can log in")
    (smoke "non-admin caller denied"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Admin-only operations (auth RPC + later admin UI):
         - create_user(username, is_admin?)
         - add_credential(user_id, kind, public_data,
           secret_data)
         - list_users / lookup_user
         - revoke_credential / revoke_session (later)

         First admin continues to come from
         bootstrap-admin env var on empty users table.
         All later humans and service accounts go through
         this section. Credentials supported in order:
         ssh_key (v1), password (with web-login), api_key
         (later).

         Enforcement: caller must present an admin session
         (token or supervisor-held admin capability).
         auth/lib/admin_api.ml is the implementation home.
    |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section supervisor
  (doc "The root unikernel: fleet management, A/B rollback, health checks, admin HTTP")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|plan-pass #4 (it20): supervisor/lib/{boot,fleet,health,process,store,ssh,auth_client,admin}.ml + supervisor/bin/supervisor.ml shipped it4. 5 admin routes served via httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html (no Dream). Boots cleanly on temp PG cluster, runs migrations, serves fleet dashboard/health/fleet JSON. End-to-end SSH→auth→image supervision verified by /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py through scripts/dev.sh. Partial (not implemented): Solo5 target — currently mirage/unix only (deferred per [architecture.mirage-vs-unix]). A/B rollback /fleet/:id/rollback route NOT built (Fleet only has boot/kill/list). VM-request boot path NOT built — Fleet spawns via posix_spawn only (dev model). See BORGE-1783433175.|) *)
  (subsection unikernel-target
   (doc "Solo5 in production, mirage/unix in dev")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it20: PARTIAL — currently mirage/unix only. The Process module (supervisor/lib/process.ml) abstracts boot behind a boot()/kill() interface; in dev that's posix_spawn over a configured PP_IMAGE_BIN path. The Solo5/hypervisor-VM-request path is NOT built — Solo5 target itself is deferred per spec [architecture.mirage-vs-unix] ("Solo5 is a build switch, not a rewrite"). The abstraction boundary the spec describes (one interface, two implementations) is in place in process.ml. No real switchover has been exercised.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The supervisor IS a unikernel, not a daemon. It
         boots directly on the hypervisor (Solo5 target)
         and manages the rest. No OpenBSD userland in the
         hot path; the hypervisor trusts exactly one thing.

         Process model: the supervisor does NOT posix_spawn
         child images (Solo5 has no spawn). Instead it
         requests new VMs from the hypervisor for each
         per-user image instance. This is the Solo5/KVM
         model: boot a unikernel = request a VM.

         In dev: mirage/unix target, supervisor runs as a
         normal process on the dev box, images too. Same
         code, different target. The fleet/process module
         abstracts the boot mechanism (VM-request in prod,
         posix_spawn in dev) behind one interface.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection fleet
   (doc "Managing per-user image instances")
   (implements supervisor/lib/fleet.ml supervisor/lib/process.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it20: PARTIAL. IMPLEMENTED — supervisor/lib/fleet.ml maintains an in-memory instance table keyed by instance_id (inst-001, -002, ...). boot/kill/list/find_by_user/mark_health operations work; ports allocated sequentially (http_port_base=18080, repl_port_base=17878) per Fleet.spawn. Verified by /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py: SSH→auth→image spawn flow registers inst-001 with the verified user_id. NOT IMPLEMENTED — fleet table persistence to PG sessions table (currently in-memory only; supervisor reboot loses it), rollback(instance_id, to_version) A/B op (no route), always-on boot_mode pin (only the boot-on-connect default exists by behavior). The spec text's `(user, branch, image_version, ..., boot_mode, last_seen)` columns are partially populated (instance_id, user_id, ports, started_at mapped; branch/image_version/boot_mode/last_seen omitted).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The supervisor maintains a fleet table:
           (user, branch, image_version, instance_id,
            boot_mode, health_status, started_at,
            last_seen)

         Operations:
         - boot(user, branch, image_version) -> instance
           On SSH connect (default boot-on-connect) or
           always-on pin.
         - kill(instance_id)
           Stop a wedged or unwanted image.
         - list-fleet() -> all instances + health
         - rollback(instance_id, to_version)
           A/B rollback for ONE user's image, not the
           whole system.

         Lifecycle:
         - boot-on-connect: image boots when user SSHes in,
           dies when session ends. Default. Doesn't persist
           in-memory REPL state across disconnects, but
           the store survives.
         - always-on: image pinned running. Power users
           who want long-lived REPL state across SSH
           disconnects. RAM always committed.

         The supervisor itself never holds user-visible
         state in memory beyond the fleet table (which is
         also in Postgres and recoverable). It's stateless
         w.r.t. user data; Postgres is the truth.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection health
   (doc "Pinging images, marking healthy/unhealthy")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it20: PARTIAL. IMPLEMENTED — supervisor/lib/health.ml is an HTTP /health client (plain Lwt_unix socket + minimal HTTP/1.0 GET). Parses the JSON response via the Protocol module (the { ok, branch, uptime, error } shape is shared with common/lib/protocol.ml and used by both the supervisor's own /health and the image's /health). Fleet.mark_health updates an instance's health_status field. NOT IMPLEMENTED — periodic background health-check loop (every 10s). Currently health is checked on-demand (e.g. before returning Fleet.list), not via a background Lwt.async tick. Unhealthy-then-autorestart logic for always-on instances is NOT built (no always-on mode to autorestart).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The supervisor health-checks each running image:
         - Periodically (every 10s) ping the image's HTTP
           health endpoint.
         - Response within timeout -> healthy.
         - No response in N tries -> unhealthy.
         - Unhealthy X seconds -> supervisor kills and
           either restarts (if always-on) or marks dead
           (if boot-on-connect; will reboot on next SSH).

         Health check protocol (in common/lib/protocol.ml):
           GET /health -> { ok: true, branch: ..., uptime: ... }
                       or { ok: false, error: ... }

         On boot of a new image version:
         - Boot image v(N+1) alongside vN (or instead of,
           simpler).
         - Wait for health-check to pass within T seconds.
         - If healthy: v(N+1) becomes new default.
         - If not: rollback to vN, log failure, alert admin.

         This makes "user upgraded primitives, broke their
         image" a 10-second recovery, not a crisis.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection admin-http
   (doc "Minimal admin surface, httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html, no Dream")
   (implements supervisor/lib/admin.ml supervisor/lib/boot.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it20/it31: PARTIAL. IMPLEMENTED — supervisor/lib/admin.ml
     serves via httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html (no Dream, consistent with
     image/). Three routes live: GET / (fleet dashboard), GET /fleet
     (JSON), GET /health (supervisor health JSON). Explicit content-length
     headers. NOT IMPLEMENTED — POST /fleet/:id/kill and
     POST /fleet/:id/rollback?to=vN; admin auth. Doctrinal text updated
     it31 away from httpaf-mirage fiction.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The supervisor exposes a small admin surface over
         HTTP via httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html (same v1
         stack as the image). Hand-written router.

         v1 SHIPPED routes:
           GET  /                  fleet dashboard
           GET  /fleet             list instances + health (JSON)
           GET  /health            supervisor's own health

         DEFERRED routes:
           POST /fleet/:id/kill
           POST /fleet/:id/rollback?to=vN

         UI: plain HTML tables (pure-html), minimal htmx.
         Auth (later): admin token via authd, or localhost
         only in dev. Not wyo.auth.

         Solo5 note: when the supervisor moves to Solo5,
         swap httpaf-lwt-unix for gluten-mirage + httpaf
         behind the same admin handler shape.
   |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section image
  (doc "The per-user unikernel: Lisp interpreter, REPL endpoint, htmx interface")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|it32: image still partial overall. browser-repl +
    function-editor implemented; system-browser + inspector partial;
    product-experience owns about/vocabulary/first-run. ssh still
    line-protocol default; linenoise still deferred.|) *)
  (subsection boot
   (doc "Reading the manifest, loading initial env")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|it23: image/lib/boot.ml + bin/unikernel.ml.
                    Reads manifest from env vars (PP_USER_ID,
                    PP_USERNAME, PP_BRANCH_ID, PP_BRANCH_NAME,
                    PP_IMAGE_VERSION, PP_HTTP_PORT, PP_REPL_PORT,
                    PP_DB_* env vars). PG connect (non-fatal on
                    failure).
                    load_defs_async populates env from function_defs
                    via recursive-CTE walk (it8). REPL endpoint +
                    HTTP server started async. Health endpoint /health.
                    The spec's 7th step 'marks self healthy, signals
                    supervisor' is NOT explicitly wired — supervisor
                    uses retry-on-connect backoff (it6) to detect image
                    readiness instead.
                    The spec describes the PROD flow as 'manifest fetched
                    from PG by the supervisor before booting' — v1 dev
                    flow uses env vars because boot-on-connect uses
                    posix_spawn (it4); prod Solo5 + hypervisor VM request
                    would carry the manifest forward, deferred. The 8-step
                    boot sequence is implemented; the PG-fetched manifest
                    is the prod-flow upgrade path.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_img_it7.py —   image boots + serves / and /health + REPL accepts connections"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         On boot, the image:
         1. Reads its boot manifest (from common/lib/manifest.ml
            type, fetched from Postgres by the supervisor
            before booting).
         2. Manifest specifies:
            - user_id (who this image is; asserted by
              supervisor after auth verified the
              credential — see section auth. The image
              trusts this because supervisor+auth did the
              verification; the image does no auth itself.)
            - username (for the REPL prompt and author
              tracking)
            - branch_id (default branch, usually user/main)
            - boot_mode ('on-connect' | 'always-on')
            - image_version (for A/B tracking)
            - config (budgets, feature flags)
         3. Loads all function_defs and type_defs for
            branch_id from Postgres into the in-memory
            env (or, with no-caching policy, sets up the
            branch-aware lookup that reads on each call).
         4. Starts the REPL endpoint (internal line
            protocol; the supervisor forwards SSH channels
            here — see ssh-interface). NOT a full SSH server.
         5. Starts the htmx server (httpaf-lwt-unix in v1)
            on configured port.
         6. Registers health endpoint.
         7. Marks self healthy, signals supervisor.

         The image is STATELESS across reboots w.r.t. user
         data: anything that must survive is in Postgres.
         In-memory REPL state (define'd locals in a
         session) does NOT survive reboot unless the user
         persisted via (store) or similar.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection ssh-interface
   (doc "The SSH front door: supervisor authenticates, forwards to image REPL")
   (implements supervisor/lib/ssh.ml supervisor/lib/ssh_awa.ml supervisor/lib/ssh_awa_shims.ml supervisor/lib/ssh_keys.ml supervisor/lib/boot.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it23: the supervisor-fronts-SSH pattern is implemented
                    (supervisor/lib/ssh.ml). v1 uses a non-crypto line
                    protocol on PP_SSH_PORT (default 17822 dev / 2222 prod):
                    accept TCP, banner, read key as first line, auth via
                    authd's /verify_ssh_key HTTP RPC, DENIED or OK+
                    boot/attach image, byte-proxy TCP between SSH socket
                    and image REPL socket. Real awa-ssh path (it21) is
                    OPT-IN via PP_AWA_SSH_PORT; real SSH transport +
                    pubkey auth works end-to-end but the exec_callback's
                    REPL proxy bridge has a Cstruct<->Lwt_io bug, AND
                    awa 0.3.1's static-auth-db constraint means the awa
                    path can't propagate user identity into the image
                    (see BORGE-1783439434). The line protocol IS the
                    spec's design minus the cryptographic transport.
                    Marked partial: the cryptographic SSH handshake
                    isn't default; waiting on awa 0.5.x upgrade (its
                    auth-event callback exposes username to exec_callback).
                    Per the spec's own tradeoff section 'supervisor-to-image
                    hop is internal (trusted hypervisor network), so no
                    end-to-end SSH crypto between user terminal and image' —
                    that has held. Image does NOT run full SSH — matches spec
                    design.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — line-protocol SSH→auth→image→REPL→define→call")
    (smoke "PP_AWA_SSH_PORT=12223 + real ed25519 key in DB → awa-ssh handshake + pubkey auth"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE SSH PATH (end to end):

         1. User runs: ssh alice@pricklypear.host
         2. SUPERVISOR listens on port 22 (awa-ssh,
            Mirage-compatible) and accepts the connection.
         3. SSH handshake + key exchange (awa-ssh, pure
            OCaml, no libssh).
         4. Authentication: supervisor calls pricklypear
            authd (NOT wyo.auth — deliberate v1 choice;
            see workflow.conventions) via verify_ssh_key
            RPC to resolve the presented public key to a
            user_id. Key -> user mapping is authd's job.
         5. Supervisor boots (boot-on-connect) or attaches
            (always-on) the user's image unikernel.
         6. Supervisor opens a channel to the image's
            internal REPL endpoint (see repl_endpoint
            below) and FORWARDS the SSH channel's PTY
            stream to it. The user's terminal is now
            talking to the image's REPL.
         7. Image presents the REPL prompt (see
            repl-experience).
         8. On disconnect, supervisor notes session end;
            boot-on-connect images die, always-on images
            keep running.

         WHY SUPERVISOR-FRONTS-SSH (not each image on its
         own port):
         - One door, one auth, one image per user. Matches
           'SSH-first OS' — it's THE login, not N logins.
         - Users don't need to know their image's port.
         - authd integration happens in one place
           (supervisor), not N places (each image).
         - The image doesn't need full SSH — just a REPL
           endpoint. Simpler image.

         TRADEOFF: supervisor-to-image hop is internal
         (trusted hypervisor network), so no end-to-end
         SSH crypto between user terminal and image. For
         a personal OS on a trusted hypervisor, this is
         acceptable. If it becomes a concern, the image
         can run its own awa-ssh on an internal port and
         the supervisor does TCP forwarding of the
         encrypted SSH stream (then the image decrypts).
         Defer this until it's a felt concern.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection repl-endpoint
   (doc "Image's internal REPL endpoint, not full SSH")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|it23: image/lib/repl_endpoint.ml. TCP listener on
                    PP_REPL_PORT. Accepts a connection, prints banner
                    'Pricklypear REPL. Type :quit to exit.', prompts
                    with prompt (branch_name + '> '). Read-eval-print loop
                    driven by Lwt_io channels. Session.t IS shared across
                    connections (one Session.t per image process), so
                    `(define x 42)` in one socket persists to the next —
                    matches the it18 smoke-test pattern. Graceful disconnect
                    (it10): all post-disconnect cleanup wrapped in Lwt.catch,
                    EBADF classified as 'peer disconnected' not fatal.
                    No full SSH in the image — matches the spec's design.
                    The supervisor connects and forwards SSH-to-image TCP
                    bytes via Lwt_unix.read/write.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_persist_it8.py — define persists + /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — REPL proxy works"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The image does NOT run a full SSH server. Instead
         it exposes an internal REPL endpoint — a simple
         line protocol over a TCP socket (or Mirage
         channel) on an internal port. The supervisor
         connects to this and forwards the user's PTY
         stream.

         Protocol (minimal, framed):
           - Connect: supervisor opens TCP to image:REPL_PORT
           - Auth: none needed (internal network; the
             supervisor already authenticated the user)
           - Exchange: lines of UTF-8. User input flows
             in; REPL output flows out.
           - The image's session.ml tracks this connection
             as a session; on disconnect, session ends.

         WHY NOT FULL SSH IN THE IMAGE:
         - Removes awa-ssh dependency from the image
           (keeps image smaller; awa crypto is non-trivial).
         - One SSH implementation (in supervisor) to
           maintain, not N.
         - authd resolution stays in supervisor.

         The endpoint is in image/lib/repl_endpoint.ml.
         It owns: accepting the supervisor's connection,
         wiring PTY stream to the line editor (lineedit.ml),
         and the REPL read-eval-print loop itself.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection repl-experience
   (doc "What the user sees: prompt, multi-line, line editing, meta-commands")
   (implements image/lib/lineedit.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it23: prompt + multi-line + meta-commands + session-state
                    are implemented in image/lib/repl_endpoint.ml.
                    Currently the prompt is '<branch-name>> ' (e.g.
                    'main> '), NOT the spec's 'alice/main>' 2-token form.
                    Multi-line: the reader's is_complete is consulted so
                    a `(define square (lambda ...` spanning lines waits for
                    the closing paren. Meta-commands (define, fork, checkout,
                    merge, list-bindings) ARE implemented as Lisp special
                    forms or prims (define is the only AST-level intercept
                    since its name arg is a Var not to be evaluated before
                    binding; others are Lwt prims on the it12 ABI). Session
                    state: define persists to PG (it8); REPL locals are
                    session-local, persist across REPL socket reconnections
                    within an image's life; checkout mutates branch_id (it11).
                    NOT IMPLEMENTED: real line EDITING (image/lib/lineedit.ml
                    is a single-line stub). ocaml-linenoise is the candidate;
                    v1 just uses raw input — no cursor movement, no history,
                    no Ctrl-A/E. Editing is desired but not v1-blocking (the
                    image IS usable via a wrapper that sends prepared lines).
                    (edit name), (describe 'type), (load "file"), (help) —
                    meta-commands not yet implemented (ergonomic gaps, see
                    lisp-interpreter.primitives' agent note). Marked partial:
                    the REPL usability core + meta-commands ship; real
                    linenoise-style editing + the 4 missing meta-commands are
                    the deferred v1.5 polish layer.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — define + apply works through the REPL"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE PROMPT:
           alice/main>           -- showing user + current branch
           alice/main (def) |     -- mid-multiline-define

         READING INPUT:
         - The REPL reads s-expressions. It keeps reading
           lines until parens are balanced and strings
           closed (standard Lisp REPL behavior). The
           reader tells us 'incomplete' so we know to
           prompt for more, not to evaluate yet.
         - One REPL turn = one s-expression (which may
           span many lines).
         - Comments: ; to end of line, as in the reader.

         LINE EDITING:
         - Real line editing matters for a dev tool.
           Raw SSH PTY + a line-editing layer.
         - Library choice deferred to build time:
           ocaml-linenoise is the leading candidate
           (small, Mirage-compatible-ish). Pure OCaml.
         - Supports: cursor movement, history (up/down),
           Ctrl-A/E, backspace, delete.
         - Search/history-reverse-search: nice-to-have,
           not v1-blocking.

         EVALUATION + PRINTING:
         - Evaluate the s-expression in the session env.
         - On success: pretty-print the resulting value.
           VList -> (a b c), VLambda -> #<lambda>,
           VRef -> #<ref event:uuid>, VInt -> 42, etc.
         - On error: print the error with location if
           possible (line/col from the reader), then a
           continuation prompt. Don't kill the session.

         META-COMMANDS (Lisp forms, not a separate
         command lexer — the reader handles them, the
         eval loop recognizes them as special forms):
           (define name expr)         bind + persist to store
           (edit name)                print htmx URL for AST editor
           (fork parent child)        create a branch
           (checkout branch)         switch session's branch
           (merge source target)      reviewer flow
           (describe 'type)           show type schema
           (list-bindings)           current env
           (load "file")              eval a file's contents
           (help)                     list specials
           Any other Lisp form        evaluate + print result

         SESSION STATE:
         - (define ...) persists to the store: survives
           image reboot (loaded on next boot of the
           branch).
         - REPL-local let bindings (e.g. (let ((x 5))
           ...) at top level): session-local, not
           persisted. Like bash variables.
         - Switching branch (checkout) within a session
           keeps REPL locals but changes which function
           defs are visible. (Decision: this is the
           'working directory' model — you carry your
           locals across branch switches.)
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection htmx-interface
   (doc "httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html, routes AS functions in the store")
   (implements image/lib/http.ml auth/lib/http.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it23: htmx-interface is partially implemented.
                    SYSTEM pages (always present in OCaml): / (hello
                    world index with nav links), /health (JSON),
                    /branches (list all branch rows), /bindings (list
                    function_defs via recursive-CTE), /binding/<name>
                    (single def pretty-print), /types (list type_defs
                    via recursive-CTE). All built in image/lib/http.ml
                    using httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html with Hx.* htmx
                    attrs (it11, it14). /inspect/:id is NOT built.
                    USER pages: `(route "/path" 'handler)` prim registers
                    in view_bindings + live-updates session.routes
                    hashtable (it9). Route handler's return value is
                    dispatched in http.ml: tagged VStr ('__html__:' prefix
                    from render-html) → raw HTML via txt ~raw:true
                    (it14); bare VStr → text in <p>; anything else →
                    pretty-printed in <pre>.
                    NOT IMPLEMENTED: the htmx attribute helpers as Lisp
                    prims — (hx-get url), (hx-post url), etc. are NOT
                    Lisp prims yet (v1 emits htmx attributes directly
                    in the raw HTML string passed to render-html).
                    Matches spec principle 'routes ARE functions in the
                    store'; 'pure-html for HTML generation (use Hx.* not
                    raw strings)' is honored at the OCaml level.
                    Marked partial: /inspect/:id + (hx-get/post) Lisp
                    prims are the unbuilt pieces. Product surfaces that
                    grow out of these pages are now owned by planned
                    subsections system-browser, browser-repl,
                    function-editor, inspector (it31).|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_ui_it11.py — system pages / /branches / /bindings / /binding/<name>")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_route_it9.py — (route ...) registers + dispatches user pages")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_types_it14.py — /types page + render-html tagged VStr dispatch"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The image's htmx endpoint serves two kinds of
         pages:

         1. SYSTEM pages (always present, part of the
            OCaml image):
            /health            health check
            /inspect/:id       browse any stored value
            /branches          list + fork/merge UI
            /types             type browser
            /bindings          function browser (per branch)

         2. USER pages (defined as functions in the store):
            registered via (route "/path" fn-name).
            The route handler function takes the request
            (params, body) and returns a value the
            system renders (an HTML node, a redirect, etc).

         Routes ARE functions in the store. Adding a route
         = (route "/foo" 'foo-handler). This makes the
         user-facing UI part of the live image, editable
         via the same mechanisms as everything else.

         pure-html (the dream_html HTML generation core,
         usable WITHOUT Dream) is used for HTML generation
         (per poohstack convention; use Hx.* not raw
         strings at the OCaml layer).

         v1 SHIPPED:
         - httpaf-lwt-unix HTTP server
         - system pages listed above (except /inspect)
         - user routes via (route ...) + render-html

         DEFERRED:
         - /inspect/:id (see image.inspector)
         - (hx-get)/(hx-post) Lisp prims (authors currently
           embed htmx attrs inside render-html strings)
         - Solo5 HTTP via gluten-mirage + httpaf
         - richer system-browser chrome (own subsection)
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection primitives-layer
   (doc "image/lib/primitives.ml: the ~20 impure fns registered at boot")
   (implements image/lib/primitives.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7: demoted implemented -> partial in
    lockstep with lisp-interpreter.primitives. Registration path is real:
    image/lib/primitives.ml + crud.ml + session-control prims + env
    auto-bind of VPrim names at session-make. Interpreter stays pure
    (lisp/ tests with no services). Section is partial because the
    registered universe still includes stubs/missing prims listed under
    lisp-interpreter.primitives (list-branches VNil stub, missing query/
    describe/inspect/diff/current-branch, hx helpers incomplete, create
    returns VStr not VRef, no author-scoped CRUD filtering). Promote back
    to implemented only when that table is honest and complete-or-trimmed.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image && cd lisp && dune build && dune runtest")
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml 48 alcotest cases; lisp/test/eval_test.ml 30 pure tests"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         See lisp-interpreter.primitives for the full
         primitive list. This subsection covers the IMAGE
         layer's responsibility: registering them with
         the interpreter at boot.

         The interpreter is pure and knows nothing about
         Postgres, htmx, or SSH. The IMAGE layer:
         1. Opens a Postgres connection (v1: pgx_lwt +
            custom Io_unix shim; Solo5 later may use
            pgx_lwt_mirage behind the same Store.t).
         2. Constructs an initial env with VPrim values
            bound to names like 'get', 'create', etc.
         3. The interpreter's Prim call dispatches by name
            to the OCaml function the image registered.
         4. Each primitive handles its own transaction
            (SERIALIZABLE by default) and permission
            checks (per multi-user.permissions; author-
            scoped filtering still partial).

         This separation is what makes the interpreter
         testable without any services running (pure
         lisp/test/eval_test.ml, no DB, no HTTP).

         v1 SHIPPED registration path: real.
         DEFERRED: full prim table completeness (see
         lisp-interpreter.primitives); Solo5 pgx path.
   |))
   )
  )
